{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B6EfwAgwwSYScNpNbbfr95","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Routing by perturbation type yields clear but moderate gains,\n2\t  strongest on Visual and Physical where perturbations disrupt localized processing\n3\t  stages. Spatial expert underperforms due to layout/distractor perturbations requiring\n4\t  whole-trajectory reasoning beyond single-step LoRA correction. Universal LoRA is\n5\t  a surprisingly strong baseline (~65%) from mixed-training cross-type robustness,\n6\t  narrowing the routing advantage. Frame Meta-Brain as parameter-efficient complement\n7\t  to capacity routing: ~65% of VLA-GSE absolute gain at 5% parameters. Ablation ladder\n8\t  Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle is clean and statistically significant.\n9\t  Add mechanistic analysis section explaining per-type routing benefit asymmetry —\n10\t  turns partial spatial result into insight rather than weakness. Consider additional\n11\t  experiment combining Meta-Brain + universal LoRA residual to show routing is additive\n12\t  to uniform adaptation.\n13\t\n14\t  '\n15\timagined_experiments:\n16\t- name: LoRA Expert Training — Specialized vs Universal\n17\t  plan_id: plan_002\n18\t  description: 'Train 4 rank-16 LoRA adapters on frozen pi0: LoRA-V (visual data),\n19\t    LoRA-S (spatial data), LoRA-P (physical data), Universal (mixed all-type data).\n20\t    Evaluate each on all 7 perturbation types to obtain the cross-type performance\n21\t    matrix.\n22\t\n23\t    '\n24\t  expected_results:\n25\t    lora_v_visual_avg: 78.3\n26\t    lora_v_camera: 75.2\n27\t    lora_v_light: 82.0\n28\t    lora_v_background: 78.4\n29\t    lora_v_noise: 77.6\n30\t    lora_v_spatial_avg: 54.0\n31\t    lora_v_physical: 50.6\n32\t    lora_s_spatial_avg: 68.0\n33\t    lora_s_distractor: 69.4\n34\t    lora_s_layout: 66.6\n35\t    lora_s_visual_avg: 46.9\n36\t    lora_s_physical: 49.2\n37\t    lora_p_physical: 78.0\n38\t    lora_p_visual_avg: 47.9\n39\t    lora_p_spatial_avg: 53.7\n40\t    universal_overall: 65.4\n41\t    universal_visual_avg: 65.35\n42\t    universal_spatial_avg: 63.7\n43\t    universal_physical: 69.0\n44\t    specialization_delta_visual: 12.95\n45\t    specialization_delta_spatial: 4.3\n46\t    specialization_delta_physical: 9.0\n47\t    specialization_delta_max: 12.95\n48\t  result_rationale: 'Visual expert (LoRA-V) shows the strongest specialization (+13%\n49\t    over universal on visual types) because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise)\n50\t    corrupt early PaliGemma representations in a consistent, localized manner — rank-16\n51\t    LoRA can learn a compact correction to these early-layer distortions. Physical\n52\t    expert (LoRA-P) shows strong but smaller specialization (+9%) because robot perturbations\n53\t    (changed dynamics/mass) affect action-space outputs which are more distributed\n54\t    across layers. Spatial expert (LoRA-S) shows weak specialization (+4.3%) because\n55\t    layout and distractor perturbations fundamentally change the required trajectory\n56\t    — not just a representational shift but a different action sequence — and single-step\n57\t    LoRA correction at the representation level cannot replan a trajectory. Cross-type\n58\t    performance degrades sharply (below frozen baseline) because each specialized\n59\t    expert learns type-specific correction biases that actively harm representations\n60\t    when applied to the wrong perturbation type. Universal LoRA at 65.4% is stronger\n61\t    than expected because mixed training provides partial cross-type robustness —\n62\t    the LoRA learns an average correction that modestly helps all types without hurting\n63\t    any.\n64\t\n65\t    '\n66\t  tables:\n67\t  - name: Cross-Type Expert Performance Matrix (success rate %)\n68\t    header:\n69\t    - Method\n70\t    - camera\n71\t    - light\n72\t    - background\n73\t    - noise\n74\t    - distractor\n75\t    - layout\n76\t    - robot\n77\t    - Overall\n78\t    rows:\n79\t    - - Frozen pi0\n80\t      - 47.6\n81\t      - 56.2\n82\t      - 51.8\n83\t      - 49.4\n84\t      - 58.6\n85\t      - 57.8\n86\t      - 53.8\n87\t      - 53.6\n88\t    - - LoRA-V\n89\t      - 75.2\n90\t      - 82.0\n91\t      - 78.4\n92\t      - 77.6\n93\t      - 54.2\n94\t      - 53.8\n95\t      - 50.6\n96\t      - 67.4\n97\t    - - LoRA-S\n98\t      - 44.2\n99\t      - 50.4\n100\t      - 47.8\n101\t      - 45.0\n102\t      - 69.4\n103\t      - 66.6\n104\t      - 49.2\n105\t      - 53.2\n106\t    - - LoRA-P\n107\t      - 45.6\n108\t      - 51.2\n109\t      - 48.4\n110\t      - 46.2\n111\t      - 54.0\n112\t      - 53.4\n113\t      - 78.0\n114\t      - 53.8\n115\t    - - Universal\n116\t      - 62.8\n117\t      - 69.4\n118\t      - 66.2\n119\t      - 63.0\n120\t      - 64.8\n121\t      - 62.6\n122\t      - 69.0\n123\t      - 65.4\n124\t  - name: Specialization Delta (expert own-type minus universal same-type)\n125\t    header:\n126\t    - Coarse Type\n127\t    - Expert Own-Type\n128\t    - Universal Same-Type\n129\t    - Delta\n130\t    rows:\n131\t    - - Visual\n132\t      - 78.3\n133\t      - 65.35\n134\t      - 12.95\n135\t    - - Spatial\n136\t      - 68.0\n137\t      - 63.7\n138\t      - 4.3\n139\t    - - Physical\n140\t      - 78.0\n141\t      - 69.0\n142\t      - 9.0\n143\t  figures_description:\n144\t  - 'Heatmap (4 experts × 7 perturbation types) with success rates. Strong diagonal\n145\t    blocks visible for LoRA-V (4 visual columns bright) and LoRA-P (robot column bright).\n146\t    LoRA-S shows only moderate brightness on distractor/layout. Universal row uniformly\n147\t    mid-tone. Off-diagonal cells for specialized experts are dark (below frozen baseline),\n148\t    visually demonstrating the cost of mismatched routing.\n149\t\n150\t    '\n151\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: 3 coarse types on x-axis, bars for [Frozen, Universal, Specialized\n152\t    Expert]. Visual group shows large gap between universal and LoRA-V. Physical group\n153\t    shows moderate gap. Spatial group shows minimal gap. Frozen baseline as dashed\n154\t    horizontal line per group.\n155\t\n156\t    '\n157\t  analysis: 'The cross-type matrix is the foundational evidence for why routing matters:\n158\t    correct expert helps significantly (+9 to +13% over universal), wrong expert hurts\n159\t    severely (below frozen). This asymmetry means random or poor routing can be worse\n160\t    than no routing at all, motivating the need for accurate PAR classification. The\n161\t    weak spatial specialization is interpretable: layout and distractor perturbations\n162\t    change the goal-relevant spatial configuration, requiring trajectory-level replanning\n163\t    that a single-step representation correction cannot provide. This is a fundamental\n164\t    limitation of the LoRA-based approach for spatial perturbations, not a routing\n165\t    failure. The universal LoRA''s surprising strength (65.4%) sets a high bar for\n166\t    Meta-Brain — the routing system must do substantially better to justify 3× the\n167\t    parameters.\n168\t\n169\t    '\n170\t- name: Meta-Brain Assembly — PAR Router + LoRA Bank\n171\t  plan_id: plan_003\n172\t  description: 'Assemble PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, 2-layer MLP, initialized from\n173\t    Phase 0 weights) with 3 LoRA experts. Soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k\n174\t    p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen). End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA\n175\t    parameters (only tune PAR weights). Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\n176\t\n177\t    '\n178\t  expected_results:\n179\t    libero_plus_overall: 71.4\n180\t    per_type_camera: 71.0\n181\t    per_type_light: 77.6\n182\t    per_type_background: 74.4\n183\t    per_type_noise: 73.0\n184\t    per_type_distractor: 65.2\n185\t    per_type_layout: 64.4\n186\t    per_type_robot: 74.2\n187\t    visual_avg: 74.0\n188\t    spatial_avg: 64.8\n189\t    physical: 74.2\n190\t    gain_over_frozen: 17.8\n191\t    gain_over_universal: 6.0\n192\t    routing_diag_visual: 0.82\n193\t    routing_diag_spatial: 0.68\n194\t    routing_diag_physical: 0.8\n195\t    routing_diagonality_mean: 0.77\n196\t    hard_assignment_accuracy: 89.0\n197\t  result_rationale: 'Soft routing dilutes the correction signal compared to hard (oracle)\n198\t    routing, costing ~4% overall. The dilution is uniform across types (3-5% per type)\n199\t    because even a small weight on the wrong expert introduces harmful correction\n200\t    biases. Spatial routing diagonality is lowest (0.68) because layout/distractor\n201\t    perturbations share visual features with camera/background changes at layer 6\n202\t    — the PAR router''s operating point. Despite this, Meta-Brain still improves over\n203\t    universal on spatial (+1.1%) because even imperfect soft routing toward LoRA-S\n204\t    is better than no type awareness. Visual gain (+8.65% over universal) is large\n205\t    because high routing confidence (0.82) allows LoRA-V to dominate the correction.\n206\t    Physical gain (+5.2%) is moderate due to good routing (0.80) but more distributed\n207\t    perturbation effects. Hard assignment accuracy (89%) is lower than Phase 0 classification\n208\t    (99.3%) because the router is fine-tuned for task success, not classification\n209\t    accuracy — it learns to soften assignments when mixing provides marginal task\n210\t    benefit.\n211\t\n212\t    '\n213\t  tables:\n214\t  - name: Meta-Brain Per-Perturbation Success Rate (%)\n215\t    header:\n216\t    - Perturbation\n217\t    - Frozen\n218\t    - Universal\n219\t    - Meta-Brain\n220\t    - Gain over Universal\n221\t    rows:\n222\t    - - camera\n223\t      - 47.6\n224\t      - 62.8\n225\t      - 71.0\n226\t      - 8.2\n227\t    - - light\n228\t      - 56.2\n229\t      - 69.4\n230\t      - 77.6\n231\t      - 8.2\n232\t    - - background\n233\t      - 51.8\n234\t      - 66.2\n235\t      - 74.4\n236\t      - 8.2\n237\t    - - noise\n238\t      - 49.4\n239\t      - 63.0\n240\t      - 73.0\n241\t      - 10.0\n242\t    - - distractor\n243\t      - 58.6\n244\t      - 64.8\n245\t      - 65.2\n246\t      - 0.4\n247\t    - - layout\n248\t      - 57.8\n249\t      - 62.6\n250\t      - 64.4\n251\t      - 1.8\n252\t    - - robot\n253\t      - 53.8\n254\t      - 69.0\n255\t      - 74.2\n256\t      - 5.2\n257\t    - - Overall\n258\t      - 53.6\n259\t      - 65.4\n260\t      - 71.4\n261\t      - 6.0\n262\t  - name: PAR Soft Routing Weight Matrix (mean weights per true type)\n263\t    header:\n264\t    - True Type\n265\t    - P(LoRA-V)\n266\t    - P(LoRA-S)\n267\t    - P(LoRA-P)\n268\t    rows:\n269\t    - - Visual\n270\t      - 0.82\n271\t      - 0.06\n272\t      - 0.12\n273\t    - - Spatial\n274\t      - 0.2\n275\t      - 0.68\n276\t      - 0.12\n277\t    - - Physical\n278\t      - 0.12\n279\t      - 0.08\n280\t      - 0.8\n281\t  figures_description:\n282\t  - 'Routing weight heatmap (3×3): rows = true perturbation type, columns = routing\n283\t    weight to expert. Near-diagonal pattern visible but imperfect. Visual and Physical\n284\t    rows show strong diagonal dominance (0.82, 0.80). Spatial row is more diffuse\n285\t    (0.68 diagonal, 0.20 leak to Visual). Annotated with actual weight values in each\n286\t    cell.\n287\t\n288\t    '\n289\t  - 'Per-type success rate bar chart: 7 perturbation types on x-axis, grouped bars\n290\t    for Frozen/Universal/Meta-Brain. Clear visual separation on camera/light/background/noise\n291\t    (Meta-Brain >> Universal >> Frozen). Minimal separation on distractor/layout.\n292\t    Moderate separation on robot.\n293\t\n294\t    '\n295\t  analysis: 'Meta-Brain achieves the target ordering (Meta-Brain > Universal > Frozen\n296\t    on all 7 types) but the margin varies dramatically: +8-10% on visual types, +5%\n297\t    on physical, only +0.4-1.8% on spatial. This asymmetry is mechanistically interpretable:\n298\t    visual perturbations corrupt the representation in a way that a type-specific\n299\t    LoRA can precisely invert, while spatial perturbations require policy-level replanning\n300\t    beyond representation correction. The routing weight analysis reveals the PAR\n301\t    router appropriately lacks confidence on spatial inputs (diag 0.68), which limits\n302\t    both the benefit and the harm of routing on these types. The end-to-end routing\n303\t    fine-tuning shifts the router from pure classification toward task-success optimization,\n304\t    explaining the gap between Phase 0 accuracy (99.3%) and deployed routing accuracy\n305\t    (~89%).\n306\t\n307\t    '\n308\t- name: Oracle Routing — Upper Bound for Routing Quality\n309\t  plan_id: plan_004\n310\t  description: 'Replace PAR soft router with ground-truth perturbation type labels\n311\t    (hard 1-hot routing to the correct expert). Same LoRA experts from plan_002. Measures\n312\t    the ceiling for routing quality and quantifies PAR''s gap to perfect routing.\n313\t\n314\t    '\n315\t  expected_results:\n316\t    oracle_overall: 75.3\n317\t    oracle_camera: 75.2\n318\t    oracle_light: 82.0\n319\t    oracle_background: 78.4\n320\t    oracle_noise: 77.6\n321\t    oracle_distractor: 69.4\n322\t    oracle_layout: 66.6\n323\t    oracle_robot: 78.0\n324\t    oracle_par_gap_overall: 3.9\n325\t    oracle_par_gap_visual: 4.3\n326\t    oracle_par_gap_spatial: 3.8\n327\t    oracle_par_gap_physical: 3.8\n328\t  result_rationale: 'Oracle routing eliminates soft-routing dilution entirely — each\n329\t    perturbation type receives 100% correction from its matched expert, with zero\n330\t    contamination from mismatched experts. The 3.9% overall gap from Meta-Brain to\n331\t    oracle quantifies the cost of learned soft routing versus perfect hard routing.\n332\t    This gap arises from two sources: (1) non-zero weight on wrong experts injects\n333\t    harmful correction biases (~60% of the gap), and (2) the router''s lower confidence\n334\t    on spatial perturbations causes LoRA-S to be under-weighted even on true spatial\n335\t    inputs (~40% of the gap). The gap is moderate (not catastrophic), indicating PAR\n336\t    routing is functional but not near-perfect. Closing this gap further would require\n337\t    either better features for spatial classification at layer 6, or a hybrid routing\n338\t    scheme that uses hard routing when confidence is high and universal fallback when\n339\t    confidence is low. Note that oracle performance (75.3%) still trails VLA-GSE (81.2%)\n340\t    by 5.9%, confirming that the performance ceiling is set by pi0 backbone capacity\n341\t    and LoRA rank, not routing quality.\n342\t\n343\t    '\n344\t  tables:\n345\t  - name: Oracle vs PAR Per-Type Gap (%)\n346\t    header:\n347\t    - Perturbation\n348\t    - Oracle\n349\t    - Meta-Brain (PAR)\n350\t    - Gap\n351\t    rows:\n352\t    - - camera\n353\t      - 75.2\n354\t      - 71.0\n355\t      - 4.2\n356\t    - - light\n357\t      - 82.0\n358\t      - 77.6\n359\t      - 4.4\n360\t    - - background\n361\t      - 78.4\n362\t      - 74.4\n363\t      - 4.0\n364\t    - - noise\n365\t      - 77.6\n366\t      - 73.0\n367\t      - 4.6\n368\t    - - distractor\n369\t      - 69.4\n370\t      - 65.2\n371\t      - 4.2\n372\t    - - layout\n373\t      - 66.6\n374\t      - 64.4\n375\t      - 2.2\n376\t    - - robot\n377\t      - 78.0\n378\t      - 74.2\n379\t      - 3.8\n380\t    - - Overall\n381\t      - 75.3\n382\t      - 71.4\n383\t      - 3.9\n384\t  figures_description:\n385\t  - 'Paired bar chart: for each perturbation type, side-by-side bars for Oracle (blue)\n386\t    and Meta-Brain PAR (orange) with the gap annotated above. Layout has the smallest\n387\t    gap (2.2%) because LoRA-S is already weak regardless of routing. Noise has the\n388\t    largest gap (4.6%) because sensor noise is hardest for the router to confidently\n389\t    classify.\n390\t\n391\t    '\n392\t  analysis: 'The oracle-PAR gap of 3.9% is in the moderate range — large enough to\n393\t    matter for absolute performance but not large enough to invalidate the routing\n394\t    approach. The implication for Claim 2 is important: even with perfect routing,\n395\t    the gain over universal LoRA would be 75.3 - 65.4 = 9.9%, only ~64% more than\n396\t    Meta-Brain''s 6.0%. This means routing quality accounts for ~40% of the potential\n397\t    routing benefit left on the table. For the paper, frame this as: \"PAR captures\n398\t    ~60% of the theoretically available routing benefit, with the remaining gap attributable\n399\t    to soft-routing dilution and spatial ambiguity.\" The gap also establishes headroom\n400\t    for future router improvements (e.g., confidence-gated hard routing).\n401\t\n402\t    '\n403\t- name: Ablation Ladder — Frozen → Universal → Meta-Brain → Oracle\n404\t  plan_id: plan_005\n405\t  description: 'Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal\n406\t    LoRA (rank 16, ~2M), Meta-Brain (PAR + 3 LoRA, ~6M), Oracle routing (ground-truth\n407\t    type). Report overall and per-perturbation breakdown with statistical significance.\n408\t    Core evidence for Claim 2.\n409\t\n410\t    '\n411\t  expected_results:\n412\t    frozen_mean: 53.6\n413\t    frozen_std: 1.8\n414\t    universal_mean: 65.4\n415\t    universal_std: 2.1\n416\t    meta_brain_mean: 71.4\n417\t    meta_brain_std: 2.4\n418\t    oracle_mean: 75.3\n419\t    oracle_std: 1.9\n420\t    gain_frozen_to_universal: 11.8\n421\t    gain_universal_to_meta_brain: 6.0\n422\t    gain_meta_brain_to_oracle: 3.9\n423\t    pvalue_universal_gt_frozen: 0.0003\n424\t    pvalue_meta_brain_gt_universal: 0.008\n425\t    pvalue_oracle_gt_meta_brain: 0.021\n426\t    meta_brain_gain_over_frozen: 17.8\n427\t  result_rationale: 'The ablation ladder shows a clean monotonic ordering (Frozen\n428\t    < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle) with all adjacent pairs statistically significant\n429\t    at p < 0.05. Step sizes decrease going up the ladder: +11.8 (baseline adaptation),\n430\t    +6.0 (routing benefit), +3.9 (routing quality gap). The largest step is Frozen\n431\t    → Universal, confirming that simply adding LoRA adaptation captures most of the\n432\t    low-hanging fruit. The Universal → Meta-Brain step (+6.0%) is the headline number\n433\t    for Claim 2 — routing by perturbation type provides meaningful additional benefit\n434\t    beyond uniform adaptation, but the advantage is moderate (not dominant). The diminishing\n435\t    step sizes reflect a fundamental pattern: each successive refinement addresses\n436\t    a smaller remaining error surface. Standard deviations are 1.8-2.4%, typical for\n437\t    LIBERO-Plus with per-seed task ordering variance. Meta-Brain''s higher std (2.4%)\n438\t    reflects routing sensitivity to seed-specific perturbation distributions.\n439\t\n440\t    '\n441\t  tables:\n442\t  - name: Ablation Ladder — Overall Success Rate (%, 5 seeds)\n443\t    header:\n444\t    - Method\n445\t    - Params\n446\t    - Mean\n447\t    - Std\n448\t    - 95% CI\n449\t    - p vs. Previous\n450\t    rows:\n451\t    - - Frozen pi0\n452\t      - 0\n453\t      - 53.6\n454\t      - 1.8\n455\t      - '[51.4, 55.8]'\n456\t      - —\n457\t    - - Universal LoRA\n458\t      - ~2M\n459\t      - 65.4\n460\t      - 2.1\n461\t      - '[62.8, 68.0]'\n462\t      - <0.001\n463\t    - - Meta-Brain\n464\t      - ~6M\n465\t      - 71.4\n466\t      - 2.4\n467\t      - '[68.4, 74.4]'\n468\t      - '0.008'\n469\t    - - Oracle\n470\t      - ~6M\n471\t      - 75.3\n472\t      - 1.9\n473\t      - '[72.9, 77.7]'\n474\t      - '0.021'\n475\t  - name: Ablation Ladder — Per-Perturbation Breakdown (%, 5-seed mean)\n476\t    header:\n477\t    - Method\n478\t    - camera\n479\t    - light\n480\t    - bg\n481\t    - noise\n482\t    - distr\n483\t    - layout\n484\t    - robot\n485\t    - Overall\n486\t    rows:\n487\t    - - Frozen\n488\t      - 47.6\n489\t      - 56.2\n490\t      - 51.8\n491\t      - 49.4\n492\t      - 58.6\n493\t      - 57.8\n494\t      - 53.8\n495\t      - 53.6\n496\t    - - Universal\n497\t      - 62.8\n498\t      - 69.4\n499\t      - 66.2\n500\t      - 63.0\n501\t      - 64.8\n502\t      - 62.6\n503\t      - 69.0\n504\t      - 65.4\n505\t    - - Meta-Brain\n506\t      - 71.0\n507\t      - 77.6\n508\t      - 74.4\n509\t      - 73.0\n510\t      - 65.2\n511\t      - 64.4\n512\t      - 74.2\n513\t      - 71.4\n514\t    - - Oracle\n515\t      - 75.2\n516\t      - 82.0\n517\t      - 78.4\n518\t      - 77.6\n519\t      - 69.4\n520\t      - 66.6\n521\t      - 78.0\n522\t      - 75.3\n523\t  - name: Routing Gain Decomposition by Coarse Type\n524\t    header:\n525\t    - Coarse Type\n526\t    - Universal\n527\t    - Meta-Brain\n528\t    - Routing Gain\n529\t    - '% of Oracle Ceiling'\n530\t    rows:\n531\t    - - Visual (4 types)\n532\t      - 65.35\n533\t      - 74.0\n534\t      - 8.65\n535\t      - 66.8%\n536\t    - - Spatial (2 types)\n537\t      - 63.7\n538\t      - 64.8\n539\t      - 1.1\n540\t      - 25.6%\n541\t    - - Physical (1 type)\n542\t      - 69.0\n543\t      - 74.2\n544\t      - 5.2\n545\t      - 57.8%\n546\t    - - Overall\n547\t      - 65.4\n548\t      - 71.4\n549\t      - 6.0\n550\t      - 60.6%\n551\t  figures_description:\n552\t  - 'Stacked bar chart (the ablation ladder): 4 methods on x-axis, success rate on\n553\t    y-axis. Bars are colored in a gradient from red (Frozen) to green (Oracle). Error\n554\t    bars show ±1 std. Horizontal dashed lines at 81.2% (VLA-GSE reference) and 53.6%\n555\t    (Frozen baseline). Clear monotonic increase with decreasing step sizes. Significance\n556\t    brackets between adjacent pairs.\n557\t\n558\t    '\n559\t  - 'Per-perturbation grouped bar chart: 7 perturbation types on x-axis, 4 methods\n560\t    as grouped bars. Reveals the asymmetric routing benefit — large visual/physical\n561\t    separation between Universal and Meta-Brain bars, minimal spatial separation.\n562\t    Oracle bars highest across all types but layout/distractor gap is notably small.\n563\t\n564\t    '\n565\t  analysis: 'The ablation ladder is clean and publishable. All pairwise comparisons\n566\t    are significant. The key narrative point is that Meta-Brain captures 60.6% of\n567\t    the theoretically available routing benefit (oracle ceiling). The per-type decomposition\n568\t    reveals that routing works best where perturbations are localized (visual: 66.8%\n569\t    of ceiling captured) and least where perturbations are distributed (spatial: 25.6%).\n570\t    This naturally leads to the mechanistic analysis section in the paper explaining\n571\t    which processing stages each perturbation type disrupts. For Claim 2, the headline\n572\t    is: \"Routing by perturbation type improves over universal LoRA by 6.0 ± 2.3% (p\n573\t    = 0.008), with gains concentrated on perturbation types that disrupt localized\n574\t    processing stages.\" This reframing adds nuance rather than claiming universal\n575\t    superiority.\n576\t\n577\t    '\n578\t- name: FTM/FLA Reproduction — Visual-Only Baseline\n579\t  plan_id: plan_006\n580\t  description: 'Reproduce FTM (4K params) and FLA (4.7M params) on pi0 for visual\n581\t    perturbation types. Compare with LoRA-V and Meta-Brain to establish visual-only\n582\t    baselines and demonstrate Meta-Brain''s multi-type advantage.\n583\t\n584\t    '\n585\t  expected_results:\n586\t    ftm_camera: 69.8\n587\t    ftm_light: 76.4\n588\t    ftm_background: 72.6\n589\t    ftm_noise: 68.2\n590\t    ftm_visual_avg: 71.75\n591\t    ftm_overall: 65.3\n592\t    fla_camera: 73.8\n593\t    fla_light: 80.2\n594\t    fla_background: 76.8\n595\t    fla_noise: 75.4\n596\t    fla_visual_avg: 76.55\n597\t    fla_overall: 68.1\n598\t    lora_v_visual_avg: 78.3\n599\t    meta_brain_visual_avg: 74.0\n600\t    meta_brain_overall: 71.4\n601\t    meta_brain_advantage_over_ftm_overall: 6.1\n602\t    meta_brain_advantage_over_fla_overall: 3.3\n603\t  result_rationale: 'FTM at 4K params is remarkably parameter-efficient for visual\n604\t    perturbations (71.75% visual avg) but fundamentally cannot handle spatial/physical\n605\t    types, leaving those at frozen baseline levels and yielding only 65.3% overall.\n606\t    FLA at 4.7M params approaches LoRA-V quality on visual types (76.55% vs 78.3%)\n607\t    but also cannot generalize beyond visual. Meta-Brain''s overall advantage (71.4%\n608\t    vs 65.3-68.1%) comes entirely from spatial and physical perturbation handling.\n609\t    On visual types alone, LoRA-V (78.3%) > FLA (76.55%) > Meta-Brain routed (74.0%)\n610\t    > FTM (71.75%), showing that specialized visual methods can match or exceed routing-based\n611\t    correction for visual perturbations specifically. The key message: FTM/FLA are\n612\t    excellent visual-only solutions but cannot serve as general robustness methods.\n613\t    Meta-Brain''s value is breadth of coverage, not per-type optimality. FTM''s per-parameter\n614\t    efficiency on visual types (11.7%/0.004M = 2925%/M) dwarfs all other methods but\n615\t    is limited to a single perturbation modality.\n616\t\n617\t    '\n618\t  tables:\n619\t  - name: Visual Perturbation Methods Comparison (%)\n620\t    header:\n621\t    - Method\n622\t    - Params\n623\t    - camera\n624\t    - light\n625\t    - bg\n626\t    - noise\n627\t    - Visual Avg\n628\t    - Overall\n629\t    rows:\n630\t    - - FTM\n631\t      - 4K\n632\t      - 69.8\n633\t      - 76.4\n634\t      - 72.6\n635\t      - 68.2\n636\t      - 71.75\n637\t      - 65.3\n638\t    - - FLA\n639\t      - 4.7M\n640\t      - 73.8\n641\t      - 80.2\n642\t      - 76.8\n643\t      - 75.4\n644\t      - 76.55\n645\t      - 68.1\n646\t    - - LoRA-V\n647\t      - ~2M\n648\t      - 75.2\n649\t      - 82.0\n650\t      - 78.4\n651\t      - 77.6\n652\t      - 78.3\n653\t      - 67.4\n654\t    - - Meta-Brain\n655\t      - ~6M\n656\t      - 71.0\n657\t      - 77.6\n658\t      - 74.4\n659\t      - 73.0\n660\t      - 74.0\n661\t      - 71.4\n662\t    - - Universal\n663\t      - ~2M\n664\t      - 62.8\n665\t      - 69.4\n666\t      - 66.2\n667\t      - 63.0\n668\t      - 65.35\n669\t      - 65.4\n670\t  figures_description:\n671\t  - 'Two-panel figure. Left: visual-types-only comparison (4 visual perturbation bars\n672\t    per method). LoRA-V tops, FLA close second, Meta-Brain third, FTM fourth, Universal\n673\t    last. Right: overall LIBERO-Plus comparison. Meta-Brain tops (71.4%), FLA second\n674\t    (68.1%), LoRA-V/Universal/FTM clustered (65-67%). Shows the reversal: best visual-only\n675\t    method is not the best overall method.\n676\t\n677\t    '\n678\t  analysis: 'This experiment establishes two key points for the paper: (1) Visual-only\n679\t    methods are competitive on visual perturbations but fail as general robustness\n680\t    solutions — there is no free lunch. (2) Meta-Brain''s routing approach provides\n681\t    the best overall coverage by handling all perturbation types, even though it doesn''t\n682\t    maximize any single type. For Claim 3, FTM serves as an extreme point on the parameter-efficiency\n683\t    scatter plot: incredible visual-only efficiency but zero spatial/physical coverage.\n684\t    Meta-Brain occupies a qualitatively different position: moderate efficiency with\n685\t    universal coverage.\n686\t\n687\t    '\n688\t- name: Parameter Efficiency Analysis\n689\t  plan_id: plan_007\n690\t  description: 'Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters.\n691\t    Produce the parameter efficiency scatter plot (x=params log scale, y=gain-over-baseline).\n692\t    Include Meta-Brain, Universal LoRA, FTM, FLA, VLA-GSE (from paper, different backbone\n693\t    caveat).\n694\t\n695\t    '\n696\t  expected_results:\n697\t    meta_brain_gain: 17.8\n698\t    meta_brain_params_M: 6\n699\t    meta_brain_gain_per_M: 2.97\n700\t    universal_gain: 11.8\n701\t    universal_params_M: 2\n702\t    universal_gain_per_M: 5.9\n703\t    ftm_gain: 11.7\n704\t    ftm_params_M: 0.004\n705\t    ftm_gain_per_M: 2925.0\n706\t    fla_gain: 14.5\n707\t    fla_params_M: 4.7\n708\t    fla_gain_per_M: 3.09\n709\t    vla_gse_gain: 27.6\n710\t    vla_gse_params_M: 114\n711\t    vla_gse_gain_per_M: 0.24\n712\t    meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_gain: 64.5\n713\t    meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_params: 5.3\n714\t  result_rationale: 'Meta-Brain achieves 2.97%/M gain-per-parameter, sitting in the\n715\t    middle of the efficiency spectrum. Universal LoRA is the most efficient multi-type\n716\t    method (5.9%/M) but has a lower absolute gain ceiling. FTM is an extreme outlier\n717\t    (2925%/M) demonstrating that ultra-compact methods exist for single-type perturbations,\n718\t    but this efficiency doesn''t scale to multi-type coverage. VLA-GSE achieves the\n719\t    highest absolute gain (27.6%) but at 0.24%/M efficiency — a 12× parameter overhead\n720\t    vs Meta-Brain for 55% more gain. The headline for Claim 3: \"Meta-Brain achieves\n721\t    64.5% of VLA-GSE''s robustness improvement using 5.3% of its trainable parameters,\n722\t    demonstrating perturbation-diagnostic routing as a parameter-efficient alternative\n723\t    to capacity-based expert routing.\" The different-backbone caveat (pi0 vs Qwen3-VL-4B)\n724\t    applies to absolute numbers but the efficiency ratio is still informative.\n725\t\n726\t    '\n727\t  tables:\n728\t  - name: Parameter Efficiency Comparison\n729\t    header:\n730\t    - Method\n731\t    - Backbone\n732\t    - Params (M)\n733\t    - Gain over Frozen (%)\n734\t    - Gain/M Params\n735\t    - Coverage\n736\t    rows:\n737\t    - - FTM\n738\t      - pi0\n739\t      - 0.004\n740\t      - 11.7\n741\t      - 2925.0\n742\t      - Visual only\n743\t    - - Universal LoRA\n744\t      - pi0\n745\t      - 2\n746\t      - 11.8\n747\t      - 5.9\n748\t      - All types\n749\t    - - FLA\n750\t      - pi0\n751\t      - 4.7\n752\t      - 14.5\n753\t      - 3.09\n754\t      - Visual only\n755\t    - - Meta-Brain\n756\t      - pi0\n757\t      - 6\n758\t      - 17.8\n759\t      - 2.97\n760\t      - All types\n761\t    - - VLA-GSE†\n762\t      - Qwen3-VL-4B\n763\t      - 114\n764\t      - 27.6\n765\t      - 0.24\n766\t      - All types\n767\t  figures_description:\n768\t  - 'Scatter plot: x-axis = trainable parameters (log scale, 0.001M to 200M), y-axis\n769\t    = gain over frozen baseline (%). Each method is a labeled point with marker size\n770\t    proportional to coverage (all-type methods larger). FTM is far left (tiny params,\n771\t    moderate gain). Universal LoRA and Meta-Brain are center-left. VLA-GSE is far\n772\t    right (high gain, huge params). A Pareto frontier line connects FTM → Universal\n773\t    → Meta-Brain, showing that Meta-Brain extends the efficiency frontier beyond universal\n774\t    adaptation. VLA-GSE sits above the frontier but at 19× the cost. Dashed diagonal\n775\t    lines show iso-efficiency contours (%/M). Annotation: \"Meta-Brain: 65% of VLA-GSE\n776\t    gain at 5% parameters.\" VLA-GSE point marked with † for different backbone.\n777\t\n778\t    '\n779\t  analysis: 'The scatter plot is the visual centerpiece of Claim 3. The key insight\n780\t    is that Meta-Brain extends the Pareto frontier established by Universal LoRA —\n781\t    it proves that routing-based specialization extracts more gain per parameter than\n782\t    simply increasing uniform LoRA rank. The framing should be \"complementary to capacity\n783\t    routing, not replacement\": Meta-Brain operates in a different region of the parameter-efficiency\n784\t    space than VLA-GSE. For resource-constrained deployment (edge devices, real-time\n785\t    control), Meta-Brain offers a practical operating point. An ideal system might\n786\t    compose both: Meta-Brain for type-aware correction + capacity routing for residual\n787\t    error.\n788\t\n789\t    '\n790\t- name: Correction Memory Ablation — With/Without CM\n791\t  plan_id: plan_008\n792\t  description: 'Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM modulates\n793\t    routing weights using retrieved exemplars from a FAISS-indexed memory bank. Quantify\n794\t    marginal gain overall and per perturbation type.\n795\t\n796\t    '\n797\t  expected_results:\n798\t    meta_brain_no_cm: 71.4\n799\t    meta_brain_with_cm: 72.6\n800\t    cm_ablation_delta: 1.2\n801\t    cm_delta_visual: 0.8\n802\t    cm_delta_spatial: 0.4\n803\t    cm_delta_physical: 2.8\n804\t    cm_alpha_learned: 0.23\n805\t    cm_retrieval_latency_ms: 1.4\n806\t  result_rationale: 'CM provides a marginal +1.2% overall gain, concentrated on physical\n807\t    perturbations (+2.8%) where the same robot dynamics changes recur consistently\n808\t    across episodes — exemplar retrieval identifies \"I''ve seen this perturbation\n809\t    before\" and sharpens the routing weight toward LoRA-P. Visual gain is small (+0.8%)\n810\t    because the PAR router already routes visual perturbations confidently (diag 0.82)\n811\t    and CM''s modulation has little room to improve. Spatial gain is minimal (+0.4%)\n812\t    because spatial perturbations are fundamentally different each time (different\n813\t    object positions) so retrieved exemplars provide weak signal. The learned alpha\n814\t    parameter (0.23) confirms the system''s own assessment: CM contributes ~23% weighting\n815\t    to the final routing decision, indicating it''s a secondary correction to PAR,\n816\t    not a primary signal. The 1.4ms retrieval latency adds negligible overhead to\n817\t    the 10Hz control loop. CM is worth including as an optional module but does not\n818\t    change the core story.\n819\t\n820\t    '\n821\t  tables:\n822\t  - name: Correction Memory Ablation (%)\n823\t    header:\n824\t    - Configuration\n825\t    - Visual\n826\t    - Spatial\n827\t    - Physical\n828\t    - Overall\n829\t    rows:\n830\t    - - Meta-Brain (no CM)\n831\t      - 74.0\n832\t      - 64.8\n833\t      - 74.2\n834\t      - 71.4\n835\t    - - Meta-Brain + CM\n836\t      - 74.8\n837\t      - 65.2\n838\t      - 77.0\n839\t      - 72.6\n840\t    - - CM Delta\n841\t      - 0.8\n842\t      - 0.4\n843\t      - 2.8\n844\t      - 1.2\n845\t  figures_description:\n846\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: 3 coarse types + overall on x-axis, paired bars (with/without\n847\t    CM). Physical bar shows visible CM benefit. Visual and spatial bars nearly identical.\n848\t    Error bars from 3 seeds. The difference is within 1 std for visual/spatial but\n849\t    marginally significant for physical.\n850\t\n851\t    '\n852\t  analysis: 'CM''s marginal contribution (1.2%) is expected in the median tier. It\n853\t    validates the module as a sound idea (positive delta on all types) but not a paper-defining\n854\t    contribution. Present CM as \"optional module, +1.2% overall, primarily benefiting\n855\t    recurrent physical perturbations.\" The per-type pattern adds a small mechanistic\n856\t    insight: exemplar-based routing modulation works best when perturbations are consistent\n857\t    (physics changes) rather than combinatorially varied (spatial layouts). For the\n858\t    paper, CM goes in the ablation section with 1-2 sentences, not a dedicated section.\n859\t\n860\t    '\n861\t- name: Router Architecture Comparison — MLP vs Linear vs Attention\n862\t  plan_id: plan_009\n863\t  description: 'Compare PAR router variants on PaliGemma layer 6: (a) 2-layer MLP\n864\t    (default, ~50K params), (b) linear classifier (~6K params), (c) cross-attention\n865\t    router (~120K params). Evaluate both routing accuracy and downstream task success\n866\t    to test whether classification accuracy improvements translate to task performance.\n867\t\n868\t    '\n869\t  expected_results:\n870\t    mlp_routing_accuracy: 93.8\n871\t    mlp_task_success: 71.4\n872\t    mlp_params: 50000\n873\t    linear_routing_accuracy: 90.6\n874\t    linear_task_success: 70.2\n875\t    linear_params: 6000\n876\t    attention_routing_accuracy: 94.2\n877\t    attention_task_success: 71.2\n878\t    attention_params: 120000\n879\t    mlp_vs_linear_task_gap: 1.2\n880\t    mlp_vs_attention_task_gap: 0.2\n881\t  result_rationale: 'MLP (default) achieves 93.8% routing accuracy and 71.4% task\n882\t    success, offering the best cost/performance tradeoff. Linear router loses 3.2%\n883\t    routing accuracy, which translates to a modest 1.2% task success drop — the per-token\n884\t    representation at layer 6 is already quite separable (Phase 0 showed F1=0.993\n885\t    with MLP), so a linear boundary captures most of the structure but misses some\n886\t    nonlinear decision boundaries near cluster edges. Attention router adds 0.4% routing\n887\t    accuracy over MLP (the classes are well-separated enough that attention over token\n888\t    sequences adds minimal information beyond mean pooling) but this doesn''t translate\n889\t    to task success improvement (71.2% vs 71.4%) — the 0.4% classification gain falls\n890\t    within the noise floor of rollout evaluation. The attention router''s 2.4× parameter\n891\t    overhead is unjustified. Conclusion: MLP is the right choice, confirming the Phase\n892\t    0 finding that perturbation type is a simple classification problem at layer 6.\n893\t\n894\t    '\n895\t  tables:\n896\t  - name: Router Architecture Comparison\n897\t    header:\n898\t    - Router\n899\t    - Params\n900\t    - Routing Acc (%)\n901\t    - Task Success (%)\n902\t    - Inference Overhead (ms)\n903\t    rows:\n904\t    - - Linear\n905\t      - 6K\n906\t      - 90.6\n907\t      - 70.2\n908\t      - 0.02\n909\t    - - MLP (default)\n910\t      - 50K\n911\t      - 93.8\n912\t      - 71.4\n913\t      - 0.05\n914\t    - - Cross-Attention\n915\t      - 120K\n916\t      - 94.2\n917\t      - 71.2\n918\t      - 0.18\n919\t  figures_description:\n920\t  - 'Dual-axis scatter: x = routing classification accuracy (%), left y = task success\n921\t    (%), right y = router parameter count. Three points (Linear, MLP, Attention).\n922\t    Shows that routing accuracy improvements beyond ~93% yield diminishing task success\n923\t    returns. The gap between routing accuracy and task success is largest for Attention,\n924\t    illustrating the disconnect between classification metrics and control metrics.\n925\t\n926\t    '\n927\t  analysis: 'This experiment addresses reviewer concern C1 (\"Phase 0 classification\n928\t    is too easy\") from a different angle: yes, the classification is relatively easy\n929\t    (even a linear classifier gets 90.6%), but the task-success gap between 90.6%\n930\t    and 93.8% routing accuracy is 1.2% — confirming that routing accuracy does matter\n931\t    at the margins. The MLP''s 3.2% classification advantage over linear translates\n932\t    to 1.2% task success gain, roughly 1:0.37 ratio. Beyond 93.8%, additional classification\n933\t    gains have near-zero marginal task benefit. This helps calibrate the oracle-PAR\n934\t    gap analysis: the 3.9% task gap from oracle routing quality corresponds to moving\n935\t    from ~93.8% to ~100% classification accuracy, and the nonlinear mapping suggests\n936\t    diminishing returns as routing approaches perfection.\n937\t\n938\t    '\n939\t- name: 6-Class vs 3-Class Routing Comparison\n940\t  plan_id: plan_010\n941\t  description: 'Compare fine-grained 6-class routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot,\n942\t    6 LoRA experts ~12M params) with coarse 3-class routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical,\n943\t    3 experts ~6M). Tests whether finer-grained perturbation-type routing justifies\n944\t    additional parameters and architectural complexity.\n945\t\n946\t    '\n947\t  expected_results:\n948\t    three_class_overall: 71.4\n949\t    three_class_routing_accuracy: 93.8\n950\t    three_class_params_M: 6\n951\t    six_class_overall: 72.2\n952\t    six_class_routing_accuracy: 89.4\n953\t    six_class_params_M: 12\n954\t    delta_overall: 0.8\n955\t    six_class_camera: 72.8\n956\t    six_class_light: 79.2\n957\t    six_class_background: 76.0\n958\t    six_class_noise: 74.6\n959\t    six_class_distractor: 66.0\n960\t    six_class_layout: 65.2\n961\t    six_class_robot: 81.6\n962\t  result_rationale: '6-class routing provides only +0.8% overall improvement over\n963\t    3-class while doubling the parameter budget (12M vs 6M) and dropping routing accuracy\n964\t    by 4.4% (89.4% vs 93.8%). The marginal gain arises primarily from the robot expert\n965\t    (+3.8% from dedicated robot LoRA vs shared Physical LoRA) where the 1-type Physical\n966\t    class benefits from fully dedicated parameters. Intra-Visual perturbation types\n967\t    (camera/light/background/noise) do NOT benefit significantly from separate experts\n968\t    because they share the same failure mode (early representation corruption) — Phase\n969\t    0 6-class analysis already showed high intra-Visual similarity. The spatial split\n970\t    (distractor/layout) gains marginally because both types require trajectory-level\n971\t    reasoning that LoRA cannot provide. The 4.4% routing accuracy drop with 6 classes\n972\t    partially offsets any per-expert specialization gain: more classes means more\n973\t    routing errors, and each error routes to a more narrowly specialized (thus more\n974\t    harmful when mismatched) expert. Net assessment: 3-class is the better design\n975\t    point.\n976\t\n977\t    '\n978\t  tables:\n979\t  - name: 6-Class vs 3-Class Comparison (%)\n980\t    header:\n981\t    - Configuration\n982\t    - Classes\n983\t    - Params\n984\t    - Routing Acc\n985\t    - Overall\n986\t    - Δ vs 3-class\n987\t    rows:\n988\t    - - 3-class Meta-Brain\n989\t      - 3\n990\t      - ~6M\n991\t      - 93.8\n992\t      - 71.4\n993\t      - —\n994\t    - - 6-class Meta-Brain\n995\t      - 6\n996\t      - ~12M\n997\t      - 89.4\n998\t      - 72.2\n999\t      - 0.8\n1000\t  - name: 6-Class Per-Type Breakdown vs 3-Class (%)\n1001\t    header:\n1002\t    - Perturbation\n1003\t    - 3-class\n1004\t    - 6-class\n1005\t    - Delta\n1006\t    rows:\n1007\t    - - camera\n1008\t      - 71.0\n1009\t      - 72.8\n1010\t      - 1.8\n1011\t    - - light\n1012\t      - 77.6\n1013\t      - 79.2\n1014\t      - 1.6\n1015\t    - - background\n1016\t      - 74.4\n1017\t      - 76.0\n1018\t      - 1.6\n1019\t    - - noise\n1020\t      - 73.0\n1021\t      - 74.6\n1022\t      - 1.6\n1023\t    - - distractor\n1024\t      - 65.2\n1025\t      - 66.0\n1026\t      - 0.8\n1027\t    - - layout\n1028\t      - 64.4\n1029\t      - 65.2\n1030\t      - 0.8\n1031\t    - - robot\n1032\t      - 74.2\n1033\t      - 81.6\n1034\t      - 7.4\n1035\t  figures_description:\n1036\t  - 'Stacked cost-benefit bar: left bar = 3-class (6M params, 71.4% success), right\n1037\t    bar = 6-class (12M params, 72.2% success). The 2× parameter cost is visually dominant\n1038\t    for a barely visible success rate gain. Annotation: \"+0.8% for 2× params = poor\n1039\t    ROI.\"\n1040\t\n1041\t    '\n1042\t  analysis: 'This directly addresses reviewer concern C5 (\"3-class taxonomy may be\n1043\t    too coarse\"). The answer is: 3-class is nearly optimal for this architecture.\n1044\t    The 6-class robot improvement (+7.4%) is notable in isolation but doesn''t offset\n1045\t    the systemic costs of finer routing. The Phase 0 intra-Visual similarity finding\n1046\t    (6-class accuracy 99.82% but intra-Visual confusion is near-zero) predicted this\n1047\t    outcome: types that cluster together in activation space don''t need separate\n1048\t    experts. Include this as a 1-paragraph ablation result: \"Finer 6-class routing\n1049\t    adds +0.8% at 2× parameters, confirming that the 3-class taxonomy aligns with\n1050\t    the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing stages.\"\n1051\t\n1052\t    '\n1053\t- name: Cross-Backbone Validation — SmolVLA\n1054\t  plan_id: plan_011\n1055\t  description: 'Replicate Phase 0 classification + Phase 1 routing pipeline on SmolVLA\n1056\t    to test whether perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes beyond pi0. SmolVLA\n1057\t    uses a different vision encoder (SigLIP) and language backbone (SmolLM), testing\n1058\t    architecture independence.\n1059\t\n1060\t    '\n1061\t  expected_results:\n1062\t    smolvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.938\n1063\t    smolvla_phase0_best_layer: 4\n1064\t    smolvla_frozen_baseline: 46.2\n1065\t    smolvla_universal_lora: 56.8\n1066\t    smolvla_meta_brain: 62.4\n1067\t    smolvla_gain_over_frozen: 16.2\n1068\t    smolvla_gain_over_universal: 5.6\n1069\t    smolvla_routing_diagonality: 0.73\n1070\t    smolvla_visual_specialization_delta: 10.4\n1071\t    smolvla_spatial_specialization_delta: 2.8\n1072\t    smolvla_physical_specialization_delta: 7.6\n1073\t  result_rationale: 'SmolVLA confirms the core finding: frozen VLA activations encode\n1074\t    perturbation type (F1=0.938, lower than pi0''s 0.993 due to SigLIP''s different\n1075\t    feature structure but still high). The best classification layer shifts to layer\n1076\t    4 (SmolVLA has fewer layers than PaliGemma). Meta-Brain on SmolVLA achieves +16.2%\n1077\t    gain over frozen (comparable to pi0''s +17.8%) and +5.6% over universal (comparable\n1078\t    to pi0''s +6.0%), demonstrating that the routing benefit is architecture-independent.\n1079\t    Absolute numbers are lower (62.4% vs 71.4%) due to SmolVLA''s weaker backbone\n1080\t    capacity (SmolLM-360M vs pi0''s larger model). The per-type specialization pattern\n1081\t    replicates: visual shows the largest delta (10.4%), physical moderate (7.6%),\n1082\t    spatial weak (2.8%). Spatial underperformance persists across backbones, confirming\n1083\t    it''s a fundamental limitation of single-step LoRA correction for trajectory-level\n1084\t    perturbations, not an artifact of pi0''s architecture. Routing diagonality (0.73)\n1085\t    is slightly lower than pi0 (0.77), reflecting SigLIP''s less structured intermediate\n1086\t    representations for perturbation classification.\n1087\t\n1088\t    '\n1089\t  tables:\n1090\t  - name: Cross-Backbone Comparison (pi0 vs SmolVLA)\n1091\t    header:\n1092\t    - Metric\n1093\t    - pi0\n1094\t    - SmolVLA\n1095\t    rows:\n1096\t    - - Phase 0 3-class F1\n1097\t      - 0.993\n1098\t      - 0.938\n1099\t    - - Best classification layer\n1100\t      - 6\n1101\t      - 4\n1102\t    - - Frozen baseline (%)\n1103\t      - 53.6\n1104\t      - 46.2\n1105\t    - - Universal LoRA (%)\n1106\t      - 65.4\n1107\t      - 56.8\n1108\t    - - Meta-Brain (%)\n1109\t      - 71.4\n1110\t      - 62.4\n1111\t    - - Gain over frozen (%)\n1112\t      - 17.8\n1113\t      - 16.2\n1114\t    - - Gain over universal (%)\n1115\t      - 6.0\n1116\t      - 5.6\n1117\t    - - Routing diagonality\n1118\t      - 0.77\n1119\t      - 0.73\n1120\t    - - Visual specialization delta (%)\n1121\t      - 12.95\n1122\t      - 10.4\n1123\t    - - Spatial specialization delta (%)\n1124\t      - 4.3\n1125\t      - 2.8\n1126\t  figures_description:\n1127\t  - 'Side-by-side ablation ladders for pi0 and SmolVLA. Both show the same monotonic\n1128\t    ordering (Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain) with similar step sizes. Pi0 bars are\n1129\t    uniformly higher (stronger backbone) but the relative gains are comparable. Annotation\n1130\t    highlights the consistent routing benefit across architectures.\n1131\t\n1132\t    '\n1133\t  analysis: 'This experiment addresses reviewer concern C3 (\"single backbone, limited\n1134\t    generalizability\"). The result is clearly positive: the qualitative pattern replicates\n1135\t    exactly (same per-type specialization ranking, same ablation ordering, comparable\n1136\t    relative gains). The Phase 0 F1 drop (0.993 → 0.938) is expected — SigLIP and\n1137\t    PaliGemma have different feature structures — but 0.938 is still high enough for\n1138\t    effective routing. The spatial underperformance replication across backbones strengthens\n1139\t    the mechanistic interpretation: it''s a property of the perturbation type, not\n1140\t    the backbone. For the paper, include as a validation subsection: \"Cross-backbone\n1141\t    results on SmolVLA replicate the qualitative findings, with comparable routing\n1142\t    gains (+5.6% vs +6.0% over universal), confirming that perturbation-diagnostic\n1143\t    routing is an architecture-independent principle.\" This is a P2 experiment — include\n1144\t    if time/GPU budget permits, otherwise cite the Phase 0 cross-backbone evidence\n1145\t    from SAFE/probing literature.\n1146\t\n1147\t    '\n1148\t- name: LoRA Layer Placement Ablation\n1149\t  plan_id: plan_012\n1150\t  description: 'Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid\n1151\t    (6-12), (c) late (12-16), (d) all layers. Test hypothesis that perturbation types\n1152\t    optimally correct at different processing stages.\n1153\t\n1154\t    '\n1155\t  expected_results:\n1156\t    lora_v_early: 80.2\n1157\t    lora_v_mid: 74.8\n1158\t    lora_v_late: 71.4\n1159\t    lora_v_all: 78.3\n1160\t    lora_s_early: 64.6\n1161\t    lora_s_mid: 70.2\n1162\t    lora_s_late: 66.8\n1163\t    lora_s_all: 68.0\n1164\t    lora_p_early: 72.8\n1165\t    lora_p_mid: 75.2\n1166\t    lora_p_late: 80.6\n1167\t    lora_p_all: 78.0\n1168\t    optimal_placement_v: early (2-6)\n1169\t    optimal_placement_s: mid (6-12)\n1170\t    optimal_placement_p: late (12-16)\n1171\t    optimal_combined_overall: 73.8\n1172\t    all_layer_combined_overall: 71.4\n1173\t    layer_placement_gain: 2.4\n1174\t  result_rationale: 'The layer placement results support the mechanistic hypothesis\n1175\t    with moderate effect sizes. LoRA-V benefits most from early layers (+1.9% over\n1176\t    all-layer) because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt\n1177\t    the initial visual encoding in PaliGemma''s early layers — correcting at the source\n1178\t    prevents error propagation. LoRA-S benefits from mid layers (+2.2% over all-layer)\n1179\t    because spatial reasoning (object relationships, layout understanding) is computed\n1180\t    in middle transformer layers where multi-head attention integrates spatial context\n1181\t    — though the absolute improvement remains modest due to the fundamental trajectory-level\n1182\t    limitation. LoRA-P benefits from late layers (+2.6% over all-layer) because physical\n1183\t    perturbations manifest at the action prediction stage in late layers where dynamics\n1184\t    models are implicitly computed. All-layer placement performs as a reasonable default\n1185\t    (second-best for each expert type) because the LoRA rank is distributed across\n1186\t    layers that contribute varying amounts. The combined optimal placement (type-specific\n1187\t    layers per expert) yields 73.8% overall (+2.4% over uniform all-layer 71.4%),\n1188\t    suggesting layer-aware routing is a promising extension.\n1189\t\n1190\t    '\n1191\t  tables:\n1192\t  - name: Layer Placement × Expert Type (own-type success rate %)\n1193\t    header:\n1194\t    - Layer Range\n1195\t    - LoRA-V (Visual)\n1196\t    - LoRA-S (Spatial)\n1197\t    - LoRA-P (Physical)\n1198\t    rows:\n1199\t    - - Early (2-6)\n1200\t      - 80.2\n1201\t      - 64.6\n1202\t      - 72.8\n1203\t    - - Mid (6-12)\n1204\t      - 74.8\n1205\t      - 70.2\n1206\t      - 75.2\n1207\t    - - Late (12-16)\n1208\t      - 71.4\n1209\t      - 66.8\n1210\t      - 80.6\n1211\t    - - All layers\n1212\t      - 78.3\n1213\t      - 68.0\n1214\t      - 78.0\n1215\t  - name: Optimal Layer Placement Summary\n1216\t    header:\n1217\t    - Expert\n1218\t    - Best Layers\n1219\t    - Own-Type Success\n1220\t    - Δ vs All-Layer\n1221\t    rows:\n1222\t    - - LoRA-V\n1223\t      - Early (2-6)\n1224\t      - 80.2\n1225\t      - 1.9\n1226\t    - - LoRA-S\n1227\t      - Mid (6-12)\n1228\t      - 70.2\n1229\t      - 2.2\n1230\t    - - LoRA-P\n1231\t      - Late (12-16)\n1232\t      - 80.6\n1233\t      - 2.6\n1234\t  figures_description:\n1235\t  - '3-panel line chart: one panel per expert type. X-axis = layer range (early/mid/late/all),\n1236\t    y-axis = own-type success rate. Each panel shows a different peak: LoRA-V peaks\n1237\t    early, LoRA-S peaks mid, LoRA-P peaks late. All-layer performance marked with\n1238\t    a dashed horizontal line in each panel for reference. The crossed peak pattern\n1239\t    visually demonstrates that different perturbation types optimally correct at different\n1240\t    processing stages.\n1241\t\n1242\t    '\n1243\t  analysis: 'This is a P2 experiment that adds mechanistic depth but is not essential\n1244\t    for the core claims. The crossed peak pattern (V-early, S-mid, P-late) aligns\n1245\t    with how VLA processing stages work: visual encoding → spatial reasoning → action\n1246\t    prediction. This provides evidence for the paper''s framing that perturbation\n1247\t    types disrupt different processing stages. The +2.4% gain from optimal layer placement\n1248\t    is small but suggests a natural extension: layer-aware routing that not only selects\n1249\t    the right expert but applies it at the right layers. This could close part of\n1250\t    the oracle-PAR gap (3.9%) without improving classification accuracy. For the paper,\n1251\t    include as a supplementary analysis with the crossed-peak figure — it''s visually\n1252\t    compelling and mechanistically interpretive. Do not pursue layer-specific routing\n1253\t    as a primary contribution; it''s a natural future work direction.'\n1254\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"720120cf-4fca-44f4-88d9-d657256af0dd","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:36:47.458Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Routing by perturbation type yields clear but moderate gains,\n  strongest on Visual and Physical where perturbations disrupt localized processing\n  stages. Spatial expert underperforms due to layout/distractor perturbations requiring\n  whole-trajectory reasoning beyond single-step LoRA correction. Universal LoRA is\n  a surprisingly strong baseline (~65%) from mixed-training cross-type robustness,\n  narrowing the routing advantage. Frame Meta-Brain as parameter-efficient complement\n  to capacity routing: ~65% of VLA-GSE absolute gain at 5% parameters. Ablation ladder\n  Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle is clean and statistically significant.\n  Add mechanistic analysis section explaining per-type routing benefit asymmetry —\n  turns partial spatial result into insight rather than weakness. Consider additional\n  experiment combining Meta-Brain + universal LoRA residual to show routing is additive\n  to uniform adaptation.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: LoRA Expert Training — Specialized vs Universal\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Train 4 rank-16 LoRA adapters on frozen pi0: LoRA-V (visual data),\n    LoRA-S (spatial data), LoRA-P (physical data), Universal (mixed all-type data).\n    Evaluate each on all 7 perturbation types to obtain the cross-type performance\n    matrix.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    lora_v_visual_avg: 78.3\n    lora_v_camera: 75.2\n    lora_v_light: 82.0\n    lora_v_background: 78.4\n    lora_v_noise: 77.6\n    lora_v_spatial_avg: 54.0\n    lora_v_physical: 50.6\n    lora_s_spatial_avg: 68.0\n    lora_s_distractor: 69.4\n    lora_s_layout: 66.6\n    lora_s_visual_avg: 46.9\n    lora_s_physical: 49.2\n    lora_p_physical: 78.0\n    lora_p_visual_avg: 47.9\n    lora_p_spatial_avg: 53.7\n    universal_overall: 65.4\n    universal_visual_avg: 65.35\n    universal_spatial_avg: 63.7\n    universal_physical: 69.0\n    specialization_delta_visual: 12.95\n    specialization_delta_spatial: 4.3\n    specialization_delta_physical: 9.0\n    specialization_delta_max: 12.95\n  result_rationale: 'Visual expert (LoRA-V) shows the strongest specialization (+13%\n    over universal on visual types) because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise)\n    corrupt early PaliGemma representations in a consistent, localized manner — rank-16\n    LoRA can learn a compact correction to these early-layer distortions. Physical\n    expert (LoRA-P) shows strong but smaller specialization (+9%) because robot perturbations\n    (changed dynamics/mass) affect action-space outputs which are more distributed\n    across layers. Spatial expert (LoRA-S) shows weak specialization (+4.3%) because\n    layout and distractor perturbations fundamentally change the required trajectory\n    — not just a representational shift but a different action sequence — and single-step\n    LoRA correction at the representation level cannot replan a trajectory. Cross-type\n    performance degrades sharply (below frozen baseline) because each specialized\n    expert learns type-specific correction biases that actively harm representations\n    when applied to the wrong perturbation type. Universal LoRA at 65.4% is stronger\n    than expected because mixed training provides partial cross-type robustness —\n    the LoRA learns an average correction that modestly helps all types without hurting\n    any.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Cross-Type Expert Performance Matrix (success rate %)\n    header:\n    - Method\n    - camera\n    - light\n    - background\n    - noise\n    - distractor\n    - layout\n    - robot\n    - Overall\n    rows:\n    - - Frozen pi0\n      - 47.6\n      - 56.2\n      - 51.8\n      - 49.4\n      - 58.6\n      - 57.8\n      - 53.8\n      - 53.6\n    - - LoRA-V\n      - 75.2\n      - 82.0\n      - 78.4\n      - 77.6\n      - 54.2\n      - 53.8\n      - 50.6\n      - 67.4\n    - - LoRA-S\n      - 44.2\n      - 50.4\n      - 47.8\n      - 45.0\n      - 69.4\n      - 66.6\n      - 49.2\n      - 53.2\n    - - LoRA-P\n      - 45.6\n      - 51.2\n      - 48.4\n      - 46.2\n      - 54.0\n      - 53.4\n      - 78.0\n      - 53.8\n    - - Universal\n      - 62.8\n      - 69.4\n      - 66.2\n      - 63.0\n      - 64.8\n      - 62.6\n      - 69.0\n      - 65.4\n  - name: Specialization Delta (expert own-type minus universal same-type)\n    header:\n    - Coarse Type\n    - Expert Own-Type\n    - Universal Same-Type\n    - Delta\n    rows:\n    - - Visual\n      - 78.3\n      - 65.35\n      - 12.95\n    - - Spatial\n      - 68.0\n      - 63.7\n      - 4.3\n    - - Physical\n      - 78.0\n      - 69.0\n      - 9.0\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Heatmap (4 experts × 7 perturbation types) with success rates. Strong diagonal\n    blocks visible for LoRA-V (4 visual columns bright) and LoRA-P (robot column bright).\n    LoRA-S shows only moderate brightness on distractor/layout. Universal row uniformly\n    mid-tone. Off-diagonal cells for specialized experts are dark (below frozen baseline),\n    visually demonstrating the cost of mismatched routing.\n\n    '\n  - 'Grouped bar chart: 3 coarse types on x-axis, bars for [Frozen, Universal, Specialized\n    Expert]. Visual group shows large gap between universal and LoRA-V. Physical group\n    shows moderate gap. Spatial group shows minimal gap. Frozen baseline as dashed\n    horizontal line per group.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'The cross-type matrix is the foundational evidence for why routing matters:\n    correct expert helps significantly (+9 to +13% over universal), wrong expert hurts\n    severely (below frozen). This asymmetry means random or poor routing can be worse\n    than no routing at all, motivating the need for accurate PAR classification. The\n    weak spatial specialization is interpretable: layout and distractor perturbations\n    change the goal-relevant spatial configuration, requiring trajectory-level replanning\n    that a single-step representation correction cannot provide. This is a fundamental\n    limitation of the LoRA-based approach for spatial perturbations, not a routing\n    failure. The universal LoRA''s surprising strength (65.4%) sets a high bar for\n    Meta-Brain — the routing system must do substantially better to justify 3× the\n    parameters.\n\n    '\n- name: Meta-Brain Assembly — PAR Router + LoRA Bank\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Assemble PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, 2-layer MLP, initialized from\n    Phase 0 weights) with 3 LoRA experts. Soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k\n    p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen). End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA\n    parameters (only tune PAR weights). Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    libero_plus_overall: 71.4\n    per_type_camera: 71.0\n    per_type_light: 77.6\n    per_type_background: 74.4\n    per_type_noise: 73.0\n    per_type_distractor: 65.2\n    per_type_layout: 64.4\n    per_type_robot: 74.2\n    visual_avg: 74.0\n    spatial_avg: 64.8\n    physical: 74.2\n    gain_over_frozen: 17.8\n    gain_over_universal: 6.0\n    routing_diag_visual: 0.82\n    routing_diag_spatial: 0.68\n    routing_diag_physical: 0.8\n    routing_diagonality_mean: 0.77\n    hard_assignment_accuracy: 89.0\n  result_rationale: 'Soft routing dilutes the correction signal compared to hard (oracle)\n    routing, costing ~4% overall. The dilution is uniform across types (3-5% per type)\n    because even a small weight on the wrong expert introduces harmful correction\n    biases. Spatial routing diagonality is lowest (0.68) because layout/distractor\n    perturbations share visual features with camera/background changes at layer 6\n    — the PAR router''s operating point. Despite this, Meta-Brain still improves over\n    universal on spatial (+1.1%) because even imperfect soft routing toward LoRA-S\n    is better than no type awareness. Visual gain (+8.65% over universal) is large\n    because high routing confidence (0.82) allows LoRA-V to dominate the correction.\n    Physical gain (+5.2%) is moderate due to good routing (0.80) but more distributed\n    perturbation effects. Hard assignment accuracy (89%) is lower than Phase 0 classification\n    (99.3%) because the router is fine-tuned for task success, not classification\n    accuracy — it learns to soften assignments when mixing provides marginal task\n    benefit.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Meta-Brain Per-Perturbation Success Rate (%)\n    header:\n    - Perturbation\n    - Frozen\n    - Universal\n    - Meta-Brain\n    - Gain over Universal\n    rows:\n    - - camera\n      - 47.6\n      - 62.8\n      - 71.0\n      - 8.2\n    - - light\n      - 56.2\n      - 69.4\n      - 77.6\n      - 8.2\n    - - background\n      - 51.8\n      - 66.2\n      - 74.4\n      - 8.2\n    - - noise\n      - 49.4\n      - 63.0\n      - 73.0\n      - 10.0\n    - - distractor\n      - 58.6\n      - 64.8\n      - 65.2\n      - 0.4\n    - - layout\n      - 57.8\n      - 62.6\n      - 64.4\n      - 1.8\n    - - robot\n      - 53.8\n      - 69.0\n      - 74.2\n      - 5.2\n    - - Overall\n      - 53.6\n      - 65.4\n      - 71.4\n      - 6.0\n  - name: PAR Soft Routing Weight Matrix (mean weights per true type)\n    header:\n    - True Type\n    - P(LoRA-V)\n    - P(LoRA-S)\n    - P(LoRA-P)\n    rows:\n    - - Visual\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.06\n      - 0.12\n    - - Spatial\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.12\n    - - Physical\n      - 0.12\n      - 0.08\n      - 0.8\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Routing weight heatmap (3×3): rows = true perturbation type, columns = routing\n    weight to expert. Near-diagonal pattern visible but imperfect. Visual and Physical\n    rows show strong diagonal dominance (0.82, 0.80). Spatial row is more diffuse\n    (0.68 diagonal, 0.20 leak to Visual). Annotated with actual weight values in each\n    cell.\n\n    '\n  - 'Per-type success rate bar chart: 7 perturbation types on x-axis, grouped bars\n    for Frozen/Universal/Meta-Brain. Clear visual separation on camera/light/background/noise\n    (Meta-Brain >> Universal >> Frozen). Minimal separation on distractor/layout.\n    Moderate separation on robot.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Meta-Brain achieves the target ordering (Meta-Brain > Universal > Frozen\n    on all 7 types) but the margin varies dramatically: +8-10% on visual types, +5%\n    on physical, only +0.4-1.8% on spatial. This asymmetry is mechanistically interpretable:\n    visual perturbations corrupt the representation in a way that a type-specific\n    LoRA can precisely invert, while spatial perturbations require policy-level replanning\n    beyond representation correction. The routing weight analysis reveals the PAR\n    router appropriately lacks confidence on spatial inputs (diag 0.68), which limits\n    both the benefit and the harm of routing on these types. The end-to-end routing\n    fine-tuning shifts the router from pure classification toward task-success optimization,\n    explaining the gap between Phase 0 accuracy (99.3%) and deployed routing accuracy\n    (~89%).\n\n    '\n- name: Oracle Routing — Upper Bound for Routing Quality\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Replace PAR soft router with ground-truth perturbation type labels\n    (hard 1-hot routing to the correct expert). Same LoRA experts from plan_002. Measures\n    the ceiling for routing quality and quantifies PAR''s gap to perfect routing.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    oracle_overall: 75.3\n    oracle_camera: 75.2\n    oracle_light: 82.0\n    oracle_background: 78.4\n    oracle_noise: 77.6\n    oracle_distractor: 69.4\n    oracle_layout: 66.6\n    oracle_robot: 78.0\n    oracle_par_gap_overall: 3.9\n    oracle_par_gap_visual: 4.3\n    oracle_par_gap_spatial: 3.8\n    oracle_par_gap_physical: 3.8\n  result_rationale: 'Oracle routing eliminates soft-routing dilution entirely — each\n    perturbation type receives 100% correction from its matched expert, with zero\n    contamination from mismatched experts. The 3.9% overall gap from Meta-Brain to\n    oracle quantifies the cost of learned soft routing versus perfect hard routing.\n    This gap arises from two sources: (1) non-zero weight on wrong experts injects\n    harmful correction biases (~60% of the gap), and (2) the router''s lower confidence\n    on spatial perturbations causes LoRA-S to be under-weighted even on true spatial\n    inputs (~40% of the gap). The gap is moderate (not catastrophic), indicating PAR\n    routing is functional but not near-perfect. Closing this gap further would require\n    either better features for spatial classification at layer 6, or a hybrid routing\n    scheme that uses hard routing when confidence is high and universal fallback when\n    confidence is low. Note that oracle performance (75.3%) still trails VLA-GSE (81.2%)\n    by 5.9%, confirming that the performance ceiling is set by pi0 backbone capacity\n    and LoRA rank, not routing quality.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Oracle vs PAR Per-Type Gap (%)\n    header:\n    - Perturbation\n    - Oracle\n    - Meta-Brain (PAR)\n    - Gap\n    rows:\n    - - camera\n      - 75.2\n      - 71.0\n      - 4.2\n    - - light\n      - 82.0\n      - 77.6\n      - 4.4\n    - - background\n      - 78.4\n      - 74.4\n      - 4.0\n    - - noise\n      - 77.6\n      - 73.0\n      - 4.6\n    - - distractor\n      - 69.4\n      - 65.2\n      - 4.2\n    - - layout\n      - 66.6\n      - 64.4\n      - 2.2\n    - - robot\n      - 78.0\n      - 74.2\n      - 3.8\n    - - Overall\n      - 75.3\n      - 71.4\n      - 3.9\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Paired bar chart: for each perturbation type, side-by-side bars for Oracle (blue)\n    and Meta-Brain PAR (orange) with the gap annotated above. Layout has the smallest\n    gap (2.2%) because LoRA-S is already weak regardless of routing. Noise has the\n    largest gap (4.6%) because sensor noise is hardest for the router to confidently\n    classify.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'The oracle-PAR gap of 3.9% is in the moderate range — large enough to\n    matter for absolute performance but not large enough to invalidate the routing\n    approach. The implication for Claim 2 is important: even with perfect routing,\n    the gain over universal LoRA would be 75.3 - 65.4 = 9.9%, only ~64% more than\n    Meta-Brain''s 6.0%. This means routing quality accounts for ~40% of the potential\n    routing benefit left on the table. For the paper, frame this as: \"PAR captures\n    ~60% of the theoretically available routing benefit, with the remaining gap attributable\n    to soft-routing dilution and spatial ambiguity.\" The gap also establishes headroom\n    for future router improvements (e.g., confidence-gated hard routing).\n\n    '\n- name: Ablation Ladder — Frozen → Universal → Meta-Brain → Oracle\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal\n    LoRA (rank 16, ~2M), Meta-Brain (PAR + 3 LoRA, ~6M), Oracle routing (ground-truth\n    type). Report overall and per-perturbation breakdown with statistical significance.\n    Core evidence for Claim 2.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    frozen_mean: 53.6\n    frozen_std: 1.8\n    universal_mean: 65.4\n    universal_std: 2.1\n    meta_brain_mean: 71.4\n    meta_brain_std: 2.4\n    oracle_mean: 75.3\n    oracle_std: 1.9\n    gain_frozen_to_universal: 11.8\n    gain_universal_to_meta_brain: 6.0\n    gain_meta_brain_to_oracle: 3.9\n    pvalue_universal_gt_frozen: 0.0003\n    pvalue_meta_brain_gt_universal: 0.008\n    pvalue_oracle_gt_meta_brain: 0.021\n    meta_brain_gain_over_frozen: 17.8\n  result_rationale: 'The ablation ladder shows a clean monotonic ordering (Frozen\n    < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle) with all adjacent pairs statistically significant\n    at p < 0.05. Step sizes decrease going up the ladder: +11.8 (baseline adaptation),\n    +6.0 (routing benefit), +3.9 (routing quality gap). The largest step is Frozen\n    → Universal, confirming that simply adding LoRA adaptation captures most of the\n    low-hanging fruit. The Universal → Meta-Brain step (+6.0%) is the headline number\n    for Claim 2 — routing by perturbation type provides meaningful additional benefit\n    beyond uniform adaptation, but the advantage is moderate (not dominant). The diminishing\n    step sizes reflect a fundamental pattern: each successive refinement addresses\n    a smaller remaining error surface. Standard deviations are 1.8-2.4%, typical for\n    LIBERO-Plus with per-seed task ordering variance. Meta-Brain''s higher std (2.4%)\n    reflects routing sensitivity to seed-specific perturbation distributions.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Ablation Ladder — Overall Success Rate (%, 5 seeds)\n    header:\n    - Method\n    - Params\n    - Mean\n    - Std\n    - 95% CI\n    - p vs. Previous\n    rows:\n    - - Frozen pi0\n      - 0\n      - 53.6\n      - 1.8\n      - '[51.4, 55.8]'\n      - —\n    - - Universal LoRA\n      - ~2M\n      - 65.4\n      - 2.1\n      - '[62.8, 68.0]'\n      - <0.001\n    - - Meta-Brain\n      - ~6M\n      - 71.4\n      - 2.4\n      - '[68.4, 74.4]'\n      - '0.008'\n    - - Oracle\n      - ~6M\n      - 75.3\n      - 1.9\n      - '[72.9, 77.7]'\n      - '0.021'\n  - name: Ablation Ladder — Per-Perturbation Breakdown (%, 5-seed mean)\n    header:\n    - Method\n    - camera\n    - light\n    - bg\n    - noise\n    - distr\n    - layout\n    - robot\n    - Overall\n    rows:\n    - - Frozen\n      - 47.6\n      - 56.2\n      - 51.8\n      - 49.4\n      - 58.6\n      - 57.8\n      - 53.8\n      - 53.6\n    - - Universal\n      - 62.8\n      - 69.4\n      - 66.2\n      - 63.0\n      - 64.8\n      - 62.6\n      - 69.0\n      - 65.4\n    - - Meta-Brain\n      - 71.0\n      - 77.6\n      - 74.4\n      - 73.0\n      - 65.2\n      - 64.4\n      - 74.2\n      - 71.4\n    - - Oracle\n      - 75.2\n      - 82.0\n      - 78.4\n      - 77.6\n      - 69.4\n      - 66.6\n      - 78.0\n      - 75.3\n  - name: Routing Gain Decomposition by Coarse Type\n    header:\n    - Coarse Type\n    - Universal\n    - Meta-Brain\n    - Routing Gain\n    - '% of Oracle Ceiling'\n    rows:\n    - - Visual (4 types)\n      - 65.35\n      - 74.0\n      - 8.65\n      - 66.8%\n    - - Spatial (2 types)\n      - 63.7\n      - 64.8\n      - 1.1\n      - 25.6%\n    - - Physical (1 type)\n      - 69.0\n      - 74.2\n      - 5.2\n      - 57.8%\n    - - Overall\n      - 65.4\n      - 71.4\n      - 6.0\n      - 60.6%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Stacked bar chart (the ablation ladder): 4 methods on x-axis, success rate on\n    y-axis. Bars are colored in a gradient from red (Frozen) to green (Oracle). Error\n    bars show ±1 std. Horizontal dashed lines at 81.2% (VLA-GSE reference) and 53.6%\n    (Frozen baseline). Clear monotonic increase with decreasing step sizes. Significance\n    brackets between adjacent pairs.\n\n    '\n  - 'Per-perturbation grouped bar chart: 7 perturbation types on x-axis, 4 methods\n    as grouped bars. Reveals the asymmetric routing benefit — large visual/physical\n    separation between Universal and Meta-Brain bars, minimal spatial separation.\n    Oracle bars highest across all types but layout/distractor gap is notably small.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'The ablation ladder is clean and publishable. All pairwise comparisons\n    are significant. The key narrative point is that Meta-Brain captures 60.6% of\n    the theoretically available routing benefit (oracle ceiling). The per-type decomposition\n    reveals that routing works best where perturbations are localized (visual: 66.8%\n    of ceiling captured) and least where perturbations are distributed (spatial: 25.6%).\n    This naturally leads to the mechanistic analysis section in the paper explaining\n    which processing stages each perturbation type disrupts. For Claim 2, the headline\n    is: \"Routing by perturbation type improves over universal LoRA by 6.0 ± 2.3% (p\n    = 0.008), with gains concentrated on perturbation types that disrupt localized\n    processing stages.\" This reframing adds nuance rather than claiming universal\n    superiority.\n\n    '\n- name: FTM/FLA Reproduction — Visual-Only Baseline\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Reproduce FTM (4K params) and FLA (4.7M params) on pi0 for visual\n    perturbation types. Compare with LoRA-V and Meta-Brain to establish visual-only\n    baselines and demonstrate Meta-Brain''s multi-type advantage.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ftm_camera: 69.8\n    ftm_light: 76.4\n    ftm_background: 72.6\n    ftm_noise: 68.2\n    ftm_visual_avg: 71.75\n    ftm_overall: 65.3\n    fla_camera: 73.8\n    fla_light: 80.2\n    fla_background: 76.8\n    fla_noise: 75.4\n    fla_visual_avg: 76.55\n    fla_overall: 68.1\n    lora_v_visual_avg: 78.3\n    meta_brain_visual_avg: 74.0\n    meta_brain_overall: 71.4\n    meta_brain_advantage_over_ftm_overall: 6.1\n    meta_brain_advantage_over_fla_overall: 3.3\n  result_rationale: 'FTM at 4K params is remarkably parameter-efficient for visual\n    perturbations (71.75% visual avg) but fundamentally cannot handle spatial/physical\n    types, leaving those at frozen baseline levels and yielding only 65.3% overall.\n    FLA at 4.7M params approaches LoRA-V quality on visual types (76.55% vs 78.3%)\n    but also cannot generalize beyond visual. Meta-Brain''s overall advantage (71.4%\n    vs 65.3-68.1%) comes entirely from spatial and physical perturbation handling.\n    On visual types alone, LoRA-V (78.3%) > FLA (76.55%) > Meta-Brain routed (74.0%)\n    > FTM (71.75%), showing that specialized visual methods can match or exceed routing-based\n    correction for visual perturbations specifically. The key message: FTM/FLA are\n    excellent visual-only solutions but cannot serve as general robustness methods.\n    Meta-Brain''s value is breadth of coverage, not per-type optimality. FTM''s per-parameter\n    efficiency on visual types (11.7%/0.004M = 2925%/M) dwarfs all other methods but\n    is limited to a single perturbation modality.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Visual Perturbation Methods Comparison (%)\n    header:\n    - Method\n    - Params\n    - camera\n    - light\n    - bg\n    - noise\n    - Visual Avg\n    - Overall\n    rows:\n    - - FTM\n      - 4K\n      - 69.8\n      - 76.4\n      - 72.6\n      - 68.2\n      - 71.75\n      - 65.3\n    - - FLA\n      - 4.7M\n      - 73.8\n      - 80.2\n      - 76.8\n      - 75.4\n      - 76.55\n      - 68.1\n    - - LoRA-V\n      - ~2M\n      - 75.2\n      - 82.0\n      - 78.4\n      - 77.6\n      - 78.3\n      - 67.4\n    - - Meta-Brain\n      - ~6M\n      - 71.0\n      - 77.6\n      - 74.4\n      - 73.0\n      - 74.0\n      - 71.4\n    - - Universal\n      - ~2M\n      - 62.8\n      - 69.4\n      - 66.2\n      - 63.0\n      - 65.35\n      - 65.4\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Two-panel figure. Left: visual-types-only comparison (4 visual perturbation bars\n    per method). LoRA-V tops, FLA close second, Meta-Brain third, FTM fourth, Universal\n    last. Right: overall LIBERO-Plus comparison. Meta-Brain tops (71.4%), FLA second\n    (68.1%), LoRA-V/Universal/FTM clustered (65-67%). Shows the reversal: best visual-only\n    method is not the best overall method.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'This experiment establishes two key points for the paper: (1) Visual-only\n    methods are competitive on visual perturbations but fail as general robustness\n    solutions — there is no free lunch. (2) Meta-Brain''s routing approach provides\n    the best overall coverage by handling all perturbation types, even though it doesn''t\n    maximize any single type. For Claim 3, FTM serves as an extreme point on the parameter-efficiency\n    scatter plot: incredible visual-only efficiency but zero spatial/physical coverage.\n    Meta-Brain occupies a qualitatively different position: moderate efficiency with\n    universal coverage.\n\n    '\n- name: Parameter Efficiency Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters.\n    Produce the parameter efficiency scatter plot (x=params log scale, y=gain-over-baseline).\n    Include Meta-Brain, Universal LoRA, FTM, FLA, VLA-GSE (from paper, different backbone\n    caveat).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    meta_brain_gain: 17.8\n    meta_brain_params_M: 6\n    meta_brain_gain_per_M: 2.97\n    universal_gain: 11.8\n    universal_params_M: 2\n    universal_gain_per_M: 5.9\n    ftm_gain: 11.7\n    ftm_params_M: 0.004\n    ftm_gain_per_M: 2925.0\n    fla_gain: 14.5\n    fla_params_M: 4.7\n    fla_gain_per_M: 3.09\n    vla_gse_gain: 27.6\n    vla_gse_params_M: 114\n    vla_gse_gain_per_M: 0.24\n    meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_gain: 64.5\n    meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_params: 5.3\n  result_rationale: 'Meta-Brain achieves 2.97%/M gain-per-parameter, sitting in the\n    middle of the efficiency spectrum. Universal LoRA is the most efficient multi-type\n    method (5.9%/M) but has a lower absolute gain ceiling. FTM is an extreme outlier\n    (2925%/M) demonstrating that ultra-compact methods exist for single-type perturbations,\n    but this efficiency doesn''t scale to multi-type coverage. VLA-GSE achieves the\n    highest absolute gain (27.6%) but at 0.24%/M efficiency — a 12× parameter overhead\n    vs Meta-Brain for 55% more gain. The headline for Claim 3: \"Meta-Brain achieves\n    64.5% of VLA-GSE''s robustness improvement using 5.3% of its trainable parameters,\n    demonstrating perturbation-diagnostic routing as a parameter-efficient alternative\n    to capacity-based expert routing.\" The different-backbone caveat (pi0 vs Qwen3-VL-4B)\n    applies to absolute numbers but the efficiency ratio is still informative.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Parameter Efficiency Comparison\n    header:\n    - Method\n    - Backbone\n    - Params (M)\n    - Gain over Frozen (%)\n    - Gain/M Params\n    - Coverage\n    rows:\n    - - FTM\n      - pi0\n      - 0.004\n      - 11.7\n      - 2925.0\n      - Visual only\n    - - Universal LoRA\n      - pi0\n      - 2\n      - 11.8\n      - 5.9\n      - All types\n    - - FLA\n      - pi0\n      - 4.7\n      - 14.5\n      - 3.09\n      - Visual only\n    - - Meta-Brain\n      - pi0\n      - 6\n      - 17.8\n      - 2.97\n      - All types\n    - - VLA-GSE†\n      - Qwen3-VL-4B\n      - 114\n      - 27.6\n      - 0.24\n      - All types\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter plot: x-axis = trainable parameters (log scale, 0.001M to 200M), y-axis\n    = gain over frozen baseline (%). Each method is a labeled point with marker size\n    proportional to coverage (all-type methods larger). FTM is far left (tiny params,\n    moderate gain). Universal LoRA and Meta-Brain are center-left. VLA-GSE is far\n    right (high gain, huge params). A Pareto frontier line connects FTM → Universal\n    → Meta-Brain, showing that Meta-Brain extends the efficiency frontier beyond universal\n    adaptation. VLA-GSE sits above the frontier but at 19× the cost. Dashed diagonal\n    lines show iso-efficiency contours (%/M). Annotation: \"Meta-Brain: 65% of VLA-GSE\n    gain at 5% parameters.\" VLA-GSE point marked with † for different backbone.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'The scatter plot is the visual centerpiece of Claim 3. The key insight\n    is that Meta-Brain extends the Pareto frontier established by Universal LoRA —\n    it proves that routing-based specialization extracts more gain per parameter than\n    simply increasing uniform LoRA rank. The framing should be \"complementary to capacity\n    routing, not replacement\": Meta-Brain operates in a different region of the parameter-efficiency\n    space than VLA-GSE. For resource-constrained deployment (edge devices, real-time\n    control), Meta-Brain offers a practical operating point. An ideal system might\n    compose both: Meta-Brain for type-aware correction + capacity routing for residual\n    error.\n\n    '\n- name: Correction Memory Ablation — With/Without CM\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM modulates\n    routing weights using retrieved exemplars from a FAISS-indexed memory bank. Quantify\n    marginal gain overall and per perturbation type.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    meta_brain_no_cm: 71.4\n    meta_brain_with_cm: 72.6\n    cm_ablation_delta: 1.2\n    cm_delta_visual: 0.8\n    cm_delta_spatial: 0.4\n    cm_delta_physical: 2.8\n    cm_alpha_learned: 0.23\n    cm_retrieval_latency_ms: 1.4\n  result_rationale: 'CM provides a marginal +1.2% overall gain, concentrated on physical\n    perturbations (+2.8%) where the same robot dynamics changes recur consistently\n    across episodes — exemplar retrieval identifies \"I''ve seen this perturbation\n    before\" and sharpens the routing weight toward LoRA-P. Visual gain is small (+0.8%)\n    because the PAR router already routes visual perturbations confidently (diag 0.82)\n    and CM''s modulation has little room to improve. Spatial gain is minimal (+0.4%)\n    because spatial perturbations are fundamentally different each time (different\n    object positions) so retrieved exemplars provide weak signal. The learned alpha\n    parameter (0.23) confirms the system''s own assessment: CM contributes ~23% weighting\n    to the final routing decision, indicating it''s a secondary correction to PAR,\n    not a primary signal. The 1.4ms retrieval latency adds negligible overhead to\n    the 10Hz control loop. CM is worth including as an optional module but does not\n    change the core story.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Correction Memory Ablation (%)\n    header:\n    - Configuration\n    - Visual\n    - Spatial\n    - Physical\n    - Overall\n    rows:\n    - - Meta-Brain (no CM)\n      - 74.0\n      - 64.8\n      - 74.2\n      - 71.4\n    - - Meta-Brain + CM\n      - 74.8\n      - 65.2\n      - 77.0\n      - 72.6\n    - - CM Delta\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.4\n      - 2.8\n      - 1.2\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Grouped bar chart: 3 coarse types + overall on x-axis, paired bars (with/without\n    CM). Physical bar shows visible CM benefit. Visual and spatial bars nearly identical.\n    Error bars from 3 seeds. The difference is within 1 std for visual/spatial but\n    marginally significant for physical.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'CM''s marginal contribution (1.2%) is expected in the median tier. It\n    validates the module as a sound idea (positive delta on all types) but not a paper-defining\n    contribution. Present CM as \"optional module, +1.2% overall, primarily benefiting\n    recurrent physical perturbations.\" The per-type pattern adds a small mechanistic\n    insight: exemplar-based routing modulation works best when perturbations are consistent\n    (physics changes) rather than combinatorially varied (spatial layouts). For the\n    paper, CM goes in the ablation section with 1-2 sentences, not a dedicated section.\n\n    '\n- name: Router Architecture Comparison — MLP vs Linear vs Attention\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Compare PAR router variants on PaliGemma layer 6: (a) 2-layer MLP\n    (default, ~50K params), (b) linear classifier (~6K params), (c) cross-attention\n    router (~120K params). Evaluate both routing accuracy and downstream task success\n    to test whether classification accuracy improvements translate to task performance.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    mlp_routing_accuracy: 93.8\n    mlp_task_success: 71.4\n    mlp_params: 50000\n    linear_routing_accuracy: 90.6\n    linear_task_success: 70.2\n    linear_params: 6000\n    attention_routing_accuracy: 94.2\n    attention_task_success: 71.2\n    attention_params: 120000\n    mlp_vs_linear_task_gap: 1.2\n    mlp_vs_attention_task_gap: 0.2\n  result_rationale: 'MLP (default) achieves 93.8% routing accuracy and 71.4% task\n    success, offering the best cost/performance tradeoff. Linear router loses 3.2%\n    routing accuracy, which translates to a modest 1.2% task success drop — the per-token\n    representation at layer 6 is already quite separable (Phase 0 showed F1=0.993\n    with MLP), so a linear boundary captures most of the structure but misses some\n    nonlinear decision boundaries near cluster edges. Attention router adds 0.4% routing\n    accuracy over MLP (the classes are well-separated enough that attention over token\n    sequences adds minimal information beyond mean pooling) but this doesn''t translate\n    to task success improvement (71.2% vs 71.4%) — the 0.4% classification gain falls\n    within the noise floor of rollout evaluation. The attention router''s 2.4× parameter\n    overhead is unjustified. Conclusion: MLP is the right choice, confirming the Phase\n    0 finding that perturbation type is a simple classification problem at layer 6.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Router Architecture Comparison\n    header:\n    - Router\n    - Params\n    - Routing Acc (%)\n    - Task Success (%)\n    - Inference Overhead (ms)\n    rows:\n    - - Linear\n      - 6K\n      - 90.6\n      - 70.2\n      - 0.02\n    - - MLP (default)\n      - 50K\n      - 93.8\n      - 71.4\n      - 0.05\n    - - Cross-Attention\n      - 120K\n      - 94.2\n      - 71.2\n      - 0.18\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Dual-axis scatter: x = routing classification accuracy (%), left y = task success\n    (%), right y = router parameter count. Three points (Linear, MLP, Attention).\n    Shows that routing accuracy improvements beyond ~93% yield diminishing task success\n    returns. The gap between routing accuracy and task success is largest for Attention,\n    illustrating the disconnect between classification metrics and control metrics.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'This experiment addresses reviewer concern C1 (\"Phase 0 classification\n    is too easy\") from a different angle: yes, the classification is relatively easy\n    (even a linear classifier gets 90.6%), but the task-success gap between 90.6%\n    and 93.8% routing accuracy is 1.2% — confirming that routing accuracy does matter\n    at the margins. The MLP''s 3.2% classification advantage over linear translates\n    to 1.2% task success gain, roughly 1:0.37 ratio. Beyond 93.8%, additional classification\n    gains have near-zero marginal task benefit. This helps calibrate the oracle-PAR\n    gap analysis: the 3.9% task gap from oracle routing quality corresponds to moving\n    from ~93.8% to ~100% classification accuracy, and the nonlinear mapping suggests\n    diminishing returns as routing approaches perfection.\n\n    '\n- name: 6-Class vs 3-Class Routing Comparison\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Compare fine-grained 6-class routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot,\n    6 LoRA experts ~12M params) with coarse 3-class routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical,\n    3 experts ~6M). Tests whether finer-grained perturbation-type routing justifies\n    additional parameters and architectural complexity.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    three_class_overall: 71.4\n    three_class_routing_accuracy: 93.8\n    three_class_params_M: 6\n    six_class_overall: 72.2\n    six_class_routing_accuracy: 89.4\n    six_class_params_M: 12\n    delta_overall: 0.8\n    six_class_camera: 72.8\n    six_class_light: 79.2\n    six_class_background: 76.0\n    six_class_noise: 74.6\n    six_class_distractor: 66.0\n    six_class_layout: 65.2\n    six_class_robot: 81.6\n  result_rationale: '6-class routing provides only +0.8% overall improvement over\n    3-class while doubling the parameter budget (12M vs 6M) and dropping routing accuracy\n    by 4.4% (89.4% vs 93.8%). The marginal gain arises primarily from the robot expert\n    (+3.8% from dedicated robot LoRA vs shared Physical LoRA) where the 1-type Physical\n    class benefits from fully dedicated parameters. Intra-Visual perturbation types\n    (camera/light/background/noise) do NOT benefit significantly from separate experts\n    because they share the same failure mode (early representation corruption) — Phase\n    0 6-class analysis already showed high intra-Visual similarity. The spatial split\n    (distractor/layout) gains marginally because both types require trajectory-level\n    reasoning that LoRA cannot provide. The 4.4% routing accuracy drop with 6 classes\n    partially offsets any per-expert specialization gain: more classes means more\n    routing errors, and each error routes to a more narrowly specialized (thus more\n    harmful when mismatched) expert. Net assessment: 3-class is the better design\n    point.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 6-Class vs 3-Class Comparison (%)\n    header:\n    - Configuration\n    - Classes\n    - Params\n    - Routing Acc\n    - Overall\n    - Δ vs 3-class\n    rows:\n    - - 3-class Meta-Brain\n      - 3\n      - ~6M\n      - 93.8\n      - 71.4\n      - —\n    - - 6-class Meta-Brain\n      - 6\n      - ~12M\n      - 89.4\n      - 72.2\n      - 0.8\n  - name: 6-Class Per-Type Breakdown vs 3-Class (%)\n    header:\n    - Perturbation\n    - 3-class\n    - 6-class\n    - Delta\n    rows:\n    - - camera\n      - 71.0\n      - 72.8\n      - 1.8\n    - - light\n      - 77.6\n      - 79.2\n      - 1.6\n    - - background\n      - 74.4\n      - 76.0\n      - 1.6\n    - - noise\n      - 73.0\n      - 74.6\n      - 1.6\n    - - distractor\n      - 65.2\n      - 66.0\n      - 0.8\n    - - layout\n      - 64.4\n      - 65.2\n      - 0.8\n    - - robot\n      - 74.2\n      - 81.6\n      - 7.4\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Stacked cost-benefit bar: left bar = 3-class (6M params, 71.4% success), right\n    bar = 6-class (12M params, 72.2% success). The 2× parameter cost is visually dominant\n    for a barely visible success rate gain. Annotation: \"+0.8% for 2× params = poor\n    ROI.\"\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'This directly addresses reviewer concern C5 (\"3-class taxonomy may be\n    too coarse\"). The answer is: 3-class is nearly optimal for this architecture.\n    The 6-class robot improvement (+7.4%) is notable in isolation but doesn''t offset\n    the systemic costs of finer routing. The Phase 0 intra-Visual similarity finding\n    (6-class accuracy 99.82% but intra-Visual confusion is near-zero) predicted this\n    outcome: types that cluster together in activation space don''t need separate\n    experts. Include this as a 1-paragraph ablation result: \"Finer 6-class routing\n    adds +0.8% at 2× parameters, confirming that the 3-class taxonomy aligns with\n    the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing stages.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Cross-Backbone Validation — SmolVLA\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Replicate Phase 0 classification + Phase 1 routing pipeline on SmolVLA\n    to test whether perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes beyond pi0. SmolVLA\n    uses a different vision encoder (SigLIP) and language backbone (SmolLM), testing\n    architecture independence.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    smolvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.938\n    smolvla_phase0_best_layer: 4\n    smolvla_frozen_baseline: 46.2\n    smolvla_universal_lora: 56.8\n    smolvla_meta_brain: 62.4\n    smolvla_gain_over_frozen: 16.2\n    smolvla_gain_over_universal: 5.6\n    smolvla_routing_diagonality: 0.73\n    smolvla_visual_specialization_delta: 10.4\n    smolvla_spatial_specialization_delta: 2.8\n    smolvla_physical_specialization_delta: 7.6\n  result_rationale: 'SmolVLA confirms the core finding: frozen VLA activations encode\n    perturbation type (F1=0.938, lower than pi0''s 0.993 due to SigLIP''s different\n    feature structure but still high). The best classification layer shifts to layer\n    4 (SmolVLA has fewer layers than PaliGemma). Meta-Brain on SmolVLA achieves +16.2%\n    gain over frozen (comparable to pi0''s +17.8%) and +5.6% over universal (comparable\n    to pi0''s +6.0%), demonstrating that the routing benefit is architecture-independent.\n    Absolute numbers are lower (62.4% vs 71.4%) due to SmolVLA''s weaker backbone\n    capacity (SmolLM-360M vs pi0''s larger model). The per-type specialization pattern\n    replicates: visual shows the largest delta (10.4%), physical moderate (7.6%),\n    spatial weak (2.8%). Spatial underperformance persists across backbones, confirming\n    it''s a fundamental limitation of single-step LoRA correction for trajectory-level\n    perturbations, not an artifact of pi0''s architecture. Routing diagonality (0.73)\n    is slightly lower than pi0 (0.77), reflecting SigLIP''s less structured intermediate\n    representations for perturbation classification.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Cross-Backbone Comparison (pi0 vs SmolVLA)\n    header:\n    - Metric\n    - pi0\n    - SmolVLA\n    rows:\n    - - Phase 0 3-class F1\n      - 0.993\n      - 0.938\n    - - Best classification layer\n      - 6\n      - 4\n    - - Frozen baseline (%)\n      - 53.6\n      - 46.2\n    - - Universal LoRA (%)\n      - 65.4\n      - 56.8\n    - - Meta-Brain (%)\n      - 71.4\n      - 62.4\n    - - Gain over frozen (%)\n      - 17.8\n      - 16.2\n    - - Gain over universal (%)\n      - 6.0\n      - 5.6\n    - - Routing diagonality\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.73\n    - - Visual specialization delta (%)\n      - 12.95\n      - 10.4\n    - - Spatial specialization delta (%)\n      - 4.3\n      - 2.8\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Side-by-side ablation ladders for pi0 and SmolVLA. Both show the same monotonic\n    ordering (Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain) with similar step sizes. Pi0 bars are\n    uniformly higher (stronger backbone) but the relative gains are comparable. Annotation\n    highlights the consistent routing benefit across architectures.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'This experiment addresses reviewer concern C3 (\"single backbone, limited\n    generalizability\"). The result is clearly positive: the qualitative pattern replicates\n    exactly (same per-type specialization ranking, same ablation ordering, comparable\n    relative gains). The Phase 0 F1 drop (0.993 → 0.938) is expected — SigLIP and\n    PaliGemma have different feature structures — but 0.938 is still high enough for\n    effective routing. The spatial underperformance replication across backbones strengthens\n    the mechanistic interpretation: it''s a property of the perturbation type, not\n    the backbone. For the paper, include as a validation subsection: \"Cross-backbone\n    results on SmolVLA replicate the qualitative findings, with comparable routing\n    gains (+5.6% vs +6.0% over universal), confirming that perturbation-diagnostic\n    routing is an architecture-independent principle.\" This is a P2 experiment — include\n    if time/GPU budget permits, otherwise cite the Phase 0 cross-backbone evidence\n    from SAFE/probing literature.\n\n    '\n- name: LoRA Layer Placement Ablation\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: 'Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid\n    (6-12), (c) late (12-16), (d) all layers. Test hypothesis that perturbation types\n    optimally correct at different processing stages.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    lora_v_early: 80.2\n    lora_v_mid: 74.8\n    lora_v_late: 71.4\n    lora_v_all: 78.3\n    lora_s_early: 64.6\n    lora_s_mid: 70.2\n    lora_s_late: 66.8\n    lora_s_all: 68.0\n    lora_p_early: 72.8\n    lora_p_mid: 75.2\n    lora_p_late: 80.6\n    lora_p_all: 78.0\n    optimal_placement_v: early (2-6)\n    optimal_placement_s: mid (6-12)\n    optimal_placement_p: late (12-16)\n    optimal_combined_overall: 73.8\n    all_layer_combined_overall: 71.4\n    layer_placement_gain: 2.4\n  result_rationale: 'The layer placement results support the mechanistic hypothesis\n    with moderate effect sizes. LoRA-V benefits most from early layers (+1.9% over\n    all-layer) because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt\n    the initial visual encoding in PaliGemma''s early layers — correcting at the source\n    prevents error propagation. LoRA-S benefits from mid layers (+2.2% over all-layer)\n    because spatial reasoning (object relationships, layout understanding) is computed\n    in middle transformer layers where multi-head attention integrates spatial context\n    — though the absolute improvement remains modest due to the fundamental trajectory-level\n    limitation. LoRA-P benefits from late layers (+2.6% over all-layer) because physical\n    perturbations manifest at the action prediction stage in late layers where dynamics\n    models are implicitly computed. All-layer placement performs as a reasonable default\n    (second-best for each expert type) because the LoRA rank is distributed across\n    layers that contribute varying amounts. The combined optimal placement (type-specific\n    layers per expert) yields 73.8% overall (+2.4% over uniform all-layer 71.4%),\n    suggesting layer-aware routing is a promising extension.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Layer Placement × Expert Type (own-type success rate %)\n    header:\n    - Layer Range\n    - LoRA-V (Visual)\n    - LoRA-S (Spatial)\n    - LoRA-P (Physical)\n    rows:\n    - - Early (2-6)\n      - 80.2\n      - 64.6\n      - 72.8\n    - - Mid (6-12)\n      - 74.8\n      - 70.2\n      - 75.2\n    - - Late (12-16)\n      - 71.4\n      - 66.8\n      - 80.6\n    - - All layers\n      - 78.3\n      - 68.0\n      - 78.0\n  - name: Optimal Layer Placement Summary\n    header:\n    - Expert\n    - Best Layers\n    - Own-Type Success\n    - Δ vs All-Layer\n    rows:\n    - - LoRA-V\n      - Early (2-6)\n      - 80.2\n      - 1.9\n    - - LoRA-S\n      - Mid (6-12)\n      - 70.2\n      - 2.2\n    - - LoRA-P\n      - Late (12-16)\n      - 80.6\n      - 2.6\n  figures_description:\n  - '3-panel line chart: one panel per expert type. X-axis = layer range (early/mid/late/all),\n    y-axis = own-type success rate. Each panel shows a different peak: LoRA-V peaks\n    early, LoRA-S peaks mid, LoRA-P peaks late. All-layer performance marked with\n    a dashed horizontal line in each panel for reference. The crossed peak pattern\n    visually demonstrates that different perturbation types optimally correct at different\n    processing stages.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'This is a P2 experiment that adds mechanistic depth but is not essential\n    for the core claims. The crossed peak pattern (V-early, S-mid, P-late) aligns\n    with how VLA processing stages work: visual encoding → spatial reasoning → action\n    prediction. This provides evidence for the paper''s framing that perturbation\n    types disrupt different processing stages. The +2.4% gain from optimal layer placement\n    is small but suggests a natural extension: layer-aware routing that not only selects\n    the right expert but applies it at the right layers. This could close part of\n    the oracle-PAR gap (3.9%) without improving classification accuracy. For the paper,\n    include as a supplementary analysis with the crossed-peak figure — it''s visually\n    compelling and mechanistically interpretive. Do not pursue layer-specific routing\n    as a primary contribution; it''s a natural future work direction.'\n","numLines":1254,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1254}}}
