{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SNc8iNri3A88YyMEAsfwjk","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2024 Review Form Analysis\n\nBased on the provided content, **the complete review form, numerical rating scale, and specific evaluation dimensions are not included in this document.**\n\nThe page titled \"Instruction for Reviews\" contains only:\n\n## What IS Provided:\n\n**Review Criteria for Regular Papers:**\n- Evaluation focuses on \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\"\n- Assessment of \"objective establishment\" and \"potential for scientific and technological impact\"\n- Relevance to robotic learning\n\n**Desk Rejection Criteria:**\n- Formatting issues\n- Anonymity violations\n- Scope violations\n\n**Reviewer Qualifications:**\n- Minimum expertise in robotics, robot learning, and machine learning\n- Ideally \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences\"\n\n## What IS NOT Provided:\n\n- No numerical scoring scale (e.g., 1-10, 1-5)\n- No score level descriptions\n- No confidence score instructions\n- No structured evaluation dimensions beyond general criteria\n- No actual review form template\n\n**Conclusion:** This document outlines review *principles* and *eligibility requirements* but does not contain the actual review form mechanics, rating scales, or scoring rubrics typically used during the submission process."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"9fc5f4e4-ffaf-47d8-91f4-67361c2baf71","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:37:45.388Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":134028,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2024 Review Form Analysis\n\nBased on the provided content, **the complete review form, numerical rating scale, and specific evaluation dimensions are not included in this document.**\n\nThe page titled \"Instruction for Reviews\" contains only:\n\n## What IS Provided:\n\n**Review Criteria for Regular Papers:**\n- Evaluation focuses on \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\"\n- Assessment of \"objective establishment\" and \"potential for scientific and technological impact\"\n- Relevance to robotic learning\n\n**Desk Rejection Criteria:**\n- Formatting issues\n- Anonymity violations\n- Scope violations\n\n**Reviewer Qualifications:**\n- Minimum expertise in robotics, robot learning, and machine learning\n- Ideally \"a minimum of 3 first author publications in major robotics conferences\"\n\n## What IS NOT Provided:\n\n- No numerical scoring scale (e.g., 1-10, 1-5)\n- No score level descriptions\n- No confidence score instructions\n- No structured evaluation dimensions beyond general criteria\n- No actual review form template\n\n**Conclusion:** This document outlines review *principles* and *eligibility requirements* but does not contain the actual review form mechanics, rating scales, or scoring rubrics typically used during the submission process.","durationMs":4165,"url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QJohch65u5cAudviZwBinV","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Review Guidelines - Evaluation Criteria\n\nBased on the provided content, the guidelines outline five primary evaluation dimensions, though **no specific numerical rating scale or complete review form is included** in this document.\n\n## Evaluation Dimensions\n\nReviewers assess papers across these categories:\n\n1. **Originality**: \"Are the problems or methods new? Is the work a novel combination of well-known techniques?\" Focus includes novelty relative to prior work.\n\n2. **Quality**: \"Is the submission technically sound? Are claims well supported (e.g., by theoretical analysis or experimental results)?\"\n\n3. **Clarity**: \"Is the submission clearly written? Is it well organized?\" Papers should provide sufficient detail for expert reproduction.\n\n4. **Significance**: \"Are the results important? Are others (researchers or practitioners) likely to use the ideas or build on them?\"\n\n5. **Relevance**: \"Does the paper address a problem in robot learning? Does it apply or improve a learning-based method?\"\n\n6. **Limitations**: Papers must include a limitations section; \"honestly reported limitations\" should be appreciated rather than penalized.\n\n## Notable Absence\n\nThe document provides evaluation criteria and guidance but **does not specify a numerical scoring scale, point ranges, or concrete rating levels** (e.g., 1-5 or 1-10 scores). Reviewers receive \"technical instructions by Email via the OpenReview system,\" suggesting scoring details exist elsewhere."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"5db23cac-5759-4eab-9b7c-aa6e92baeb79","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:37:48.011Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":105095,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Review Guidelines - Evaluation Criteria\n\nBased on the provided content, the guidelines outline five primary evaluation dimensions, though **no specific numerical rating scale or complete review form is included** in this document.\n\n## Evaluation Dimensions\n\nReviewers assess papers across these categories:\n\n1. **Originality**: \"Are the problems or methods new? Is the work a novel combination of well-known techniques?\" Focus includes novelty relative to prior work.\n\n2. **Quality**: \"Is the submission technically sound? Are claims well supported (e.g., by theoretical analysis or experimental results)?\"\n\n3. **Clarity**: \"Is the submission clearly written? Is it well organized?\" Papers should provide sufficient detail for expert reproduction.\n\n4. **Significance**: \"Are the results important? Are others (researchers or practitioners) likely to use the ideas or build on them?\"\n\n5. **Relevance**: \"Does the paper address a problem in robot learning? Does it apply or improve a learning-based method?\"\n\n6. **Limitations**: Papers must include a limitations section; \"honestly reported limitations\" should be appreciated rather than penalized.\n\n## Notable Absence\n\nThe document provides evaluation criteria and guidance but **does not specify a numerical scoring scale, point ranges, or concrete rating levels** (e.g., 1-5 or 1-10 scores). Reviewers receive \"technical instructions by Email via the OpenReview system,\" suggesting scoring details exist elsewhere.","durationMs":5694,"url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CZFfkg1LEscXxkF6iPp4SG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The CoRL review form details with specific numerical scales are not publicly available on their website. They're likely embedded in the OpenReview system directly. Let me use the standard CoRL review scale based on what's commonly known. CoRL typically uses a 1-10 scale similar to other ML conferences. Let me try to find this through OpenReview or other sources.\n\nBased on my knowledge and common conference practices, CoRL uses OpenReview and typically has the following rating scale (similar to NeurIPS/ICLR):\n- 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n\n\n- 8: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold  \n- 3: Clear reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nWith an acceptance rate around 30-35%, I should verify this against an official source to be certain.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":933,"cache_read_input_tokens":70081,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":933},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"27e0105f-6ba7-49d0-801c-ecd6cb23a4de"}
