{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RjkRDP1MjgTWqAmV5NigCe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've done enough searching. Based on my knowledge and what I've found, CoRL uses a standard OpenReview-style rating scale. The CoRL acceptance rate is typically around 30-35%. Let me now compile my understanding of the CoRL review standards and proceed with the review.\n\nFrom what I know and found:\n- CoRL uses OpenReview\n- Review dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- Rating scale: typically 1-10 (similar to NeurIPS/ICLR)\n  - 10: Award-quality\n  - 8: Strong accept\n  - 6: Weak accept  \n  - 5: Borderline\n  - 4: Weak reject\n  - 3: Reject\n  - 1: Strong reject\n- Acceptance rate: ~30% (CoRL 2024 had 671 submissions)\n- Confidence: 1-5 scale\n\nNow I'll set up the review process by dispatching two specialized reviewers in parallel—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then run them through a structured roundtable with independent reviews, cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis steps.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1102,"cache_read_input_tokens":71014,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1102},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"7f096729-648c-4148-8524-402033d3c156"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RjkRDP1MjgTWqAmV5NigCe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"CoRL review standards established. Now launching two parallel reviewer agents (R1: Methods+Experiments, R2: Novelty+Related Work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1102,"cache_read_input_tokens":71014,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1102},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"9254782a-ee74-4421-974d-f0f13b777a42"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RjkRDP1MjgTWqAmV5NigCe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LQ4Te7gZVbHZuRYt6tuXWZ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- **Venue**: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning), acceptance rate ~30%\n- **Rating scale**: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=strong accept, 6=weak accept/borderline accept, 5=borderline, 4=weak reject, 3=clear reject, 1=strong reject)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (5=absolutely certain, 4=confident, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to defend, 1=educated guess)\n- **Evaluation dimensions**: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL values: real robot experiments preferred but simulation accepted for architecture contributions; relevance to robot learning community; reproducibility\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic LoRA Routing for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea**: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type (Visual/Spatial/Physical) with near-perfect accuracy at a single layer. Meta-Brain exploits this by routing corrective LoRA experts via a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR), achieving 71.4% on LIBERO-Plus with ~6M params (vs. 53.6% frozen, 65.4% universal LoRA, 75.3% oracle).\n\n**Architecture**:\n1. PAR: 2-layer MLP (~50K params) on frozen PaliGemma layer-6 mean-pooled hidden states → softmax over 3 classes\n2. ALB: 3 rank-16 LoRA adapters (Visual, Spatial, Physical), ~6M total\n3. Optional Correction Memory (CM): FAISS-indexed exemplar retrieval, learned alpha=0.23 blending\n\n**Key results**:\n- Ablation ladder: Frozen (53.6) < Universal (65.4) < Meta-Brain (71.4) < Oracle (75.3), all steps significant (p<0.05)\n- Routing gains asymmetric: Visual +8.7%, Physical +5.2%, Spatial +1.1% over universal\n- 64.5% of VLA-GSE's gain at 5.3% of params\n- Cross-backbone on SmolVLA: +5.6% over universal (comparable to pi0's +6.0%)\n- Layer placement ablation: V→early, S→mid, P→late peaks (crossed pattern)\n- 6-class vs 3-class: only +0.8% for 2× params\n- CM: +1.2% overall (marginal)\n- Router architecture: MLP best cost/performance tradeoff\n\n**Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)**:\nThe paper covers all 10 imagined experiments from the design document:\n1. Expert specialization cross-type matrix ✓\n2. Meta-Brain assembly + PAR routing ✓  \n3. Oracle routing upper bound ✓\n4. Ablation ladder with 5-seed statistics ✓\n5. FTM/FLA visual-only baseline comparison ✓\n6. Parameter efficiency analysis ✓\n7. CM ablation ✓\n8. Router architecture comparison ✓\n9. 6-class vs 3-class ✓\n10. Cross-backbone SmolVLA ✓\n11. Layer placement ablation ✓\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on **Methods** and **Experiments**. Structure your review as:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### 2. Strengths (bulleted, focus on method design and experimental rigor)\n\n### 3. Weaknesses (bulleted, focus on methodological gaps and experimental concerns)\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n### 6. Scores\n- Soundness (1-4): Is the method technically correct?\n- Empirical Rigor (1-4): Are experiments sufficient and well-controlled?\n- Reproducibility (1-4): Could someone reproduce this?\n- Overall Rating (1-10, CoRL scale)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay special attention to:\n- Whether the 3-class perturbation taxonomy is well-justified or overly simplistic\n- Whether the \"frozen activations encode perturbation type\" claim is as novel/surprising as presented\n- The VLA-GSE comparison fairness (different backbone)\n- Whether spatial underperformance is adequately addressed or is a significant weakness\n- Statistical rigor (5 seeds, p-values, confidence intervals)\n- The gap between Meta-Brain and oracle (only 60.6% of ceiling captured)\n- Whether the Correction Memory module is a meaningful contribution or padding\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1102,"cache_read_input_tokens":71014,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1102},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"aa5d7d3e-8a64-43e2-9184-3b372db9356f"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a2d7d90eefab01575","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LQ4Te7gZVbHZuRYt6tuXWZ","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- **Venue**: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning), acceptance rate ~30%\n- **Rating scale**: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=strong accept, 6=weak accept/borderline accept, 5=borderline, 4=weak reject, 3=clear reject, 1=strong reject)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (5=absolutely certain, 4=confident, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to defend, 1=educated guess)\n- **Evaluation dimensions**: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL values: real robot experiments preferred but simulation accepted for architecture contributions; relevance to robot learning community; reproducibility\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic LoRA Routing for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea**: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type (Visual/Spatial/Physical) with near-perfect accuracy at a single layer. Meta-Brain exploits this by routing corrective LoRA experts via a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR), achieving 71.4% on LIBERO-Plus with ~6M params (vs. 53.6% frozen, 65.4% universal LoRA, 75.3% oracle).\n\n**Architecture**:\n1. PAR: 2-layer MLP (~50K params) on frozen PaliGemma layer-6 mean-pooled hidden states → softmax over 3 classes\n2. ALB: 3 rank-16 LoRA adapters (Visual, Spatial, Physical), ~6M total\n3. Optional Correction Memory (CM): FAISS-indexed exemplar retrieval, learned alpha=0.23 blending\n\n**Key results**:\n- Ablation ladder: Frozen (53.6) < Universal (65.4) < Meta-Brain (71.4) < Oracle (75.3), all steps significant (p<0.05)\n- Routing gains asymmetric: Visual +8.7%, Physical +5.2%, Spatial +1.1% over universal\n- 64.5% of VLA-GSE's gain at 5.3% of params\n- Cross-backbone on SmolVLA: +5.6% over universal (comparable to pi0's +6.0%)\n- Layer placement ablation: V→early, S→mid, P→late peaks (crossed pattern)\n- 6-class vs 3-class: only +0.8% for 2× params\n- CM: +1.2% overall (marginal)\n- Router architecture: MLP best cost/performance tradeoff\n\n**Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)**:\nThe paper covers all 10 imagined experiments from the design document:\n1. Expert specialization cross-type matrix ✓\n2. Meta-Brain assembly + PAR routing ✓  \n3. Oracle routing upper bound ✓\n4. Ablation ladder with 5-seed statistics ✓\n5. FTM/FLA visual-only baseline comparison ✓\n6. Parameter efficiency analysis ✓\n7. CM ablation ✓\n8. Router architecture comparison ✓\n9. 6-class vs 3-class ✓\n10. Cross-backbone SmolVLA ✓\n11. Layer placement ablation ✓\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on **Methods** and **Experiments**. Structure your review as:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### 2. Strengths (bulleted, focus on method design and experimental rigor)\n\n### 3. Weaknesses (bulleted, focus on methodological gaps and experimental concerns)\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n### 6. Scores\n- Soundness (1-4): Is the method technically correct?\n- Empirical Rigor (1-4): Are experiments sufficient and well-controlled?\n- Reproducibility (1-4): Could someone reproduce this?\n- Overall Rating (1-10, CoRL scale)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay special attention to:\n- Whether the 3-class perturbation taxonomy is well-justified or overly simplistic\n- Whether the \"frozen activations encode perturbation type\" claim is as novel/surprising as presented\n- The VLA-GSE comparison fairness (different backbone)\n- Whether spatial underperformance is adequately addressed or is a significant weakness\n- Statistical rigor (5 seeds, p-values, confidence intervals)\n- The gap between Meta-Brain and oracle (only 60.6% of ceiling captured)\n- Whether the Correction Memory module is a meaningful contribution or padding\n","uuid":"0beb5eeb-5596-4616-a2f6-954c58b640b2","session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- **Venue**: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning), acceptance rate ~30%\n- **Rating scale**: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=strong accept, 6=weak accept/borderline accept, 5=borderline, 4=weak reject, 3=clear reject, 1=strong reject)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (5=absolutely certain, 4=confident, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to defend, 1=educated guess)\n- **Evaluation dimensions**: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL values: real robot experiments preferred but simulation accepted for architecture contributions; relevance to robot learning community; reproducibility\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic LoRA Routing for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea**: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type (Visual/Spatial/Physical) with near-perfect accuracy at a single layer. Meta-Brain exploits this by routing corrective LoRA experts via a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR), achieving 71.4% on LIBERO-Plus with ~6M params (vs. 53.6% frozen, 65.4% universal LoRA, 75.3% oracle).\n\n**Architecture**:\n1. PAR: 2-layer MLP (~50K params) on frozen PaliGemma layer-6 mean-pooled hidden states → softmax over 3 classes\n2. ALB: 3 rank-16 LoRA adapters (Visual, Spatial, Physical), ~6M total\n3. Optional Correction Memory (CM): FAISS-indexed exemplar retrieval, learned alpha=0.23 blending\n\n**Key results**:\n- Ablation ladder: Frozen (53.6) < Universal (65.4) < Meta-Brain (71.4) < Oracle (75.3), all steps significant (p<0.05)\n- Routing gains asymmetric: Visual +8.7%, Physical +5.2%, Spatial +1.1% over universal\n- 64.5% of VLA-GSE's gain at 5.3% of params\n- Cross-backbone on SmolVLA: +5.6% over universal (comparable to pi0's +6.0%)\n- Layer placement ablation: V→early, S→mid, P→late peaks (crossed pattern)\n- 6-class vs 3-class: only +0.8% for 2× params\n- CM: +1.2% overall (marginal)\n- Router architecture: MLP best cost/performance tradeoff\n\n**Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)**:\nThe paper covers all 10 imagined experiments from the design document:\n1. Expert specialization cross-type matrix ✓\n2. Meta-Brain assembly + PAR routing ✓  \n3. Oracle routing upper bound ✓\n4. Ablation ladder with 5-seed statistics ✓\n5. FTM/FLA visual-only baseline comparison ✓\n6. Parameter efficiency analysis ✓\n7. CM ablation ✓\n8. Router architecture comparison ✓\n9. 6-class vs 3-class ✓\n10. Cross-backbone SmolVLA ✓\n11. Layer placement ablation ✓\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on **Methods** and **Experiments**. Structure your review as:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### 2. Strengths (bulleted, focus on method design and experimental rigor)\n\n### 3. Weaknesses (bulleted, focus on methodological gaps and experimental concerns)\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n### 6. Scores\n- Soundness (1-4): Is the method technically correct?\n- Empirical Rigor (1-4): Are experiments sufficient and well-controlled?\n- Reproducibility (1-4): Could someone reproduce this?\n- Overall Rating (1-10, CoRL scale)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay special attention to:\n- Whether the 3-class perturbation taxonomy is well-justified or overly simplistic\n- Whether the \"frozen activations encode perturbation type\" claim is as novel/surprising as presented\n- The VLA-GSE comparison fairness (different backbone)\n- Whether spatial underperformance is adequately addressed or is a significant weakness\n- Statistical rigor (5 seeds, p-values, confidence intervals)\n- The gap between Meta-Brain and oracle (only 60.6% of ceiling captured)\n- Whether the Correction Memory module is a meaningful contribution or padding\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LQ4Te7gZVbHZuRYt6tuXWZ","session_