{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011Liab2xk9QXoVvPb49jr1J","type":"tool_result","content":"artifacts/_project/report/phase0_experiment_spec.md\nartifacts/phase0_experiment_spec.md\nlogs/workers/worker_doc_phase0-experiment-spec_20260515T110032.log\nlogs/workers/worker_doc_phase0-experiment-spec_20260515T110032_result.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/best/imagined_experiments.yaml\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/best/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/median/imagined_experiments.yaml\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/median/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/worst/imagined_experiments.yaml\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/worst/experiment_data.json\nexperiment_plan.yaml"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LQ4Te7gZVbHZuRYt6tuXWZ","session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"7f130fbe-5910-4c74-be98-e5f54781d354","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:38:58.549Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RjkRDP1MjgTWqAmV5NigCe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SCpZUDUWJBkc4eqtEYBXAg","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work Review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- **Venue**: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning), acceptance rate ~30%\n- **Rating scale**: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=strong accept, 6=weak accept/borderline accept, 5=borderline, 4=weak reject, 3=clear reject, 1=strong reject)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (5=absolutely certain, 4=confident, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to defend, 1=educated guess)\n- **Evaluation dimensions**: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL values: real robot experiments preferred but simulation accepted for architecture contributions; relevance to robot learning community; reproducibility\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic LoRA Routing for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea**: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type (Visual/Spatial/Physical) with near-perfect accuracy at a single layer. Meta-Brain exploits this by routing corrective LoRA experts via a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR), achieving 71.4% on LIBERO-Plus with ~6M params (vs. 53.6% frozen, 65.4% universal LoRA, 75.3% oracle).\n\n**Architecture**:\n1. PAR: 2-layer MLP (~50K params) on frozen PaliGemma layer-6 mean-pooled hidden states → softmax over 3 classes\n2. ALB: 3 rank-16 LoRA adapters (Visual, Spatial, Physical), ~6M total\n3. Optional Correction Memory (CM): FAISS-indexed exemplar retrieval, learned alpha=0.23\n\n**Three claimed contributions**:\n1. Empirical finding: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer granularity\n2. Architecture: Meta-Brain routing framework, 71.4% on LIBERO-Plus with ~6M params\n3. Design dimension analysis: perturbation-diagnostic routing as orthogonal axis to capacity routing\n\n**Related work coverage**:\n- VLA Robustness: VLA-GSE, RobustVLA, SRPO, CorridorVLA\n- Visual Perturbation Correction: FTM, FLA\n- Parameter-Efficient Adaptation: LoRA, LoRA-SP, QLoRA, MoIRA, CORAL\n- Perturbation Diagnosis: SAFE, OpenVLA probing, pi0.5 feature steering\n\n**Key results**:\n- 71.4% overall, +17.8% over frozen, +6.0% over universal LoRA\n- 64.5% of VLA-GSE gain at 5.3% of parameters (but different backbone caveat)\n- Cross-backbone SmolVLA replicates qualitative pattern (+5.6%)\n- Routing gains asymmetric: Visual +8.7%, Spatial +1.1%, Physical +5.2%\n- Layer placement crossed-peak pattern: V→early, S→mid, P→late\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Structure your review as:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### 2. Novelty Assessment (detailed)\n- How novel is the core observation (activations encode perturbation type)?\n- How novel is the routing architecture itself?\n- How novel is the perturbation taxonomy (Visual/Spatial/Physical)?\n- Is \"closing the diagnose-correct loop\" genuinely new or incremental combination?\n\n### 3. Related Work Gaps\n- Are there missing references or comparisons?\n- Is the positioning relative to existing work fair and accurate?\n- Consider: MoE routing literature, domain adaptation, test-time adaptation, robustness literature beyond VLAs\n\n### 4. Strengths (bulleted, focus on originality and positioning)\n\n### 5. Weaknesses (bulleted, focus on novelty limitations and related work gaps)\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n### 7. Scores\n- Novelty (1-4): How original is this work?\n- Related Work Coverage (1-4): How complete is the literature survey?\n- Significance (1-4): Will this influence future work?\n- Overall Rating (1-10, CoRL scale)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay special attention to:\n- Whether the \"frozen activations encode perturbation type\" finding is truly novel given SAFE, OpenVLA probing studies, and feature steering on pi0.5 (all cited by the paper itself)\n- Whether \"routing LoRA experts by perturbation type\" is a sufficiently novel idea versus standard MoE/LoRA routing\n- Whether the paper adequately distinguishes from MoIRA/CORAL (task routing vs perturbation routing) — is this distinction meaningful enough?\n- The framing of \"orthogonal design dimension\" — is this overstatement?\n- Whether the mechanistic analysis (layer placement, spatial limitation) constitutes genuine novelty\n- Missing comparisons to test-time adaptation methods, domain generalization, or other robustness paradigms\n- The reference list is quite short (9 references) — is this adequate?\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1102,"cache_read_input_tokens":71014,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1102},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"3fb6be65-bde2-41c1-875d-b7e1d0c13a89"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ae4bff39501736df8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01SCpZUDUWJBkc4eqtEYBXAg","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work Review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- **Venue**: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning), acceptance rate ~30%\n- **Rating scale**: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=strong accept, 6=weak accept/borderline accept, 5=borderline, 4=weak reject, 3=clear reject, 1=strong reject)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (5=absolutely certain, 4=confident, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to defend, 1=educated guess)\n- **Evaluation dimensions**: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL values: real robot experiments preferred but simulation accepted for architecture contributions; relevance to robot learning community; reproducibility\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic LoRA Routing for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Core idea**: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type (Visual/Spatial/Physical) with near-perfect accuracy at a single layer. Meta-Brain exploits this by routing corrective LoRA experts via a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR), achieving 71.4% on LIBERO-Plus with ~6M params (vs. 53.6% frozen, 65.4% universal LoRA, 75.3% oracle).\n\n**Architecture**:\n1. PAR: 2-layer MLP (~50K params) on frozen PaliGemma layer-6 mean-pooled hidden states → softmax over 3 classes\n2. ALB: 3 rank-16 LoRA adapters (Visual, Spatial, Physical), ~6M total\n3. Optional Correction Memory (CM): FAISS-indexed exemplar retrieval, learned alpha=0.23\n\n**Three claimed contributions**:\n1. Empirical finding: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer granularity\n2. Architecture: Meta-Brain routing framework, 71.4% on LIBERO-Plus with ~6M params\n3. Design dimension analysis: perturbation-diagnostic routing as orthogonal axis to capacity routing\n\n**Related work coverage**:\n- VLA Robustness: VLA-GSE, RobustVLA, SRPO, CorridorVLA\n- Visual Perturbation Correction: FTM, FLA\n- Parameter-Efficient Adaptation: LoRA, LoRA-SP, QLoRA, MoIRA, CORAL\n- Perturbation Diagnosis: SAFE, OpenVLA probing, pi0.5 feature steering\n\n**Key results**:\n- 71.4% overall, +17.8% over frozen, +6.0% over universal LoRA\n- 64.5% of VLA-GSE gain at 5.3% of parameters (but different backbone caveat)\n- Cross-backbone SmolVLA replicates qualitative pattern (+5.6%)\n- Routing gains asymmetric: Visual +8.7%, Spatial +1.1%, Physical +5.2%\n- Layer placement crossed-peak pattern: V→early, S→mid, P→late\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Structure your review as:\n\n### 1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### 2. Novelty Assessment (detailed)\n- How novel is the core observation (activations encode perturbation type)?\n- How novel is the routing architecture itself?\n- How novel is the perturbation taxonomy (Visual/Spatial/Physical)?\n- Is \"closing the diagnose-correct loop\" genuinely new or incremental combination?\n\n### 3. Related Work Gaps\n- Are there missing references or comparisons?\n- Is the positioning relative to existing work fair and accurate?\n- Consider: MoE routing literature, domain adaptation, test-time adaptation, robustness literature beyond VLAs\n\n### 4. Strengths (bulleted, focus on originality and positioning)\n\n### 5. Weaknesses (bulleted, focus on novelty limitations and related work gaps)\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n### 7. Scores\n- Novelty (1-4): How original is this work?\n- Related Work Coverage (1-4): How complete is the literature survey?\n- Significance (1-4): Will this influence future work?\n- Overall Rating (1-10, CoRL scale)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay special attention to:\n- Whether the \"frozen activations encode perturbation type\" finding is truly novel given SAFE, OpenVLA probing studies, and feature steering on pi0.5 (all cited by the paper itself)\n- Whether \"routing LoRA experts by perturbation type\" is a sufficiently novel idea versus standard MoE/LoRA routing\n- Whether the paper adequately distinguishes from MoIRA/CORAL (task routing vs perturbation routing) — is this distinction meaningful enough?\n- The framing of \"orthogonal design dimension\" — is this overstatement?\n- Whether the mechanistic analysis (layer placement, spatial limitation) constitutes genuine novelty\n- Missing comparisons to test-time adaptation methods, domain generalization, or other robustness paradigms\n- The reference list is quite short (9 references) — is this adequate?\n","uuid":"0e802484-b909-4967-a474-66ef5825cdc9","session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c"}
