{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HW5XG8HQv5kehZEPvxGhQQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\ttier: worst\n2\tnarrative_guidance: 'Paper pivots from ''routing improves correction'' to ''what VLA\n3\t  representations reveal about perturbation processing''. Lead contribution becomes\n4\t  Phase 0 mechanistic discovery (F1=1.0 at K=1 single layer). Contribution 2 becomes\n5\t  a systematic negative-but-informative result: explicit perturbation-diagnostic routing\n6\t  yields only marginal, statistically non-significant gains over implicit mixed-perturbation\n7\t  LoRA training, providing evidence that rank-16 LoRA mixed training already captures\n8\t  perturbation-conditional structure. Universal LoRA is the surprising finding — it\n9\t  implicitly learns type-aware correction without any routing signal. Title shifts\n10\t  to ''Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong? Probing Perturbation Representations and the\n11\t  Limits of Diagnostic Routing''. Key framing: the routing signal is real (Phase 0\n12\t  proves it), but the correction bottleneck is expert capacity, not routing quality.\n13\t  This reframes the contribution as a design-dimension analysis that maps out the\n14\t  landscape for future work with higher-rank experts, multi-backbone settings, or\n15\t  compositional perturbation scenarios.\n16\t\n17\t  '\n18\timagined_experiments:\n19\t- name: LoRA expert training — 3 specialized + 1 universal\n20\t  plan_id: plan_002\n21\t  description: 'Train 4 LoRA adapters (rank 16) on frozen pi0 PaliGemma: LoRA-V (Visual),\n22\t    LoRA-S (Spatial), LoRA-P (Physical), Universal (all types mixed). Evaluate each\n23\t    on all perturbation types.\n24\t\n25\t    '\n26\t  expected_results:\n27\t    lora_v_on_camera: 61.0\n28\t    lora_v_on_light: 70.0\n29\t    lora_v_on_background: 65.0\n30\t    lora_v_on_sensor_noise: 72.0\n31\t    lora_v_visual_avg: 67.0\n32\t    lora_s_on_layout: 66.0\n33\t    lora_p_on_robot: 73.0\n34\t    universal_on_camera: 57.0\n35\t    universal_on_light: 68.0\n36\t    universal_on_background: 61.0\n37\t    universal_on_sensor_noise: 70.0\n38\t    universal_on_layout: 62.0\n39\t    universal_on_robot: 70.8\n40\t    universal_overall: 64.8\n41\t    max_specialization_delta: 4.0\n42\t    lora_v_on_layout_cross: 58.0\n43\t    lora_v_on_robot_cross: 65.0\n44\t    lora_s_on_visual_avg_cross: 59.5\n45\t    lora_p_on_visual_avg_cross: 60.5\n46\t  result_rationale: 'Specialized experts show only marginal gains over universal on\n47\t    their own types (max +4.0% on background/layout, +2.0–2.2% on light/noise/robot).\n48\t    Root cause: rank-16 LoRA has limited correction capacity (~2M params per expert),\n49\t    and at this rank the parameter space is too constrained to learn meaningfully\n50\t    different type-specific behaviors. All experts converge to similar low-rank corrections\n51\t    that primarily recover the frozen backbone''s general robustness gap. The universal\n52\t    LoRA, trained on mixed perturbation data, implicitly learns a perturbation-conditional\n53\t    representation — mixed training acts as a form of implicit multi-task learning\n54\t    that covers the type-specific corrections nearly as well as explicit specialization.\n55\t    Cross-type degradation exists (LoRA-V on layout drops to 58.0 vs universal 62.0)\n56\t    but is moderate, further indicating that experts are not highly differentiated.\n57\t\n58\t    '\n59\t  tables:\n60\t  - title: 'Cross-evaluation matrix: Expert × Perturbation Type (success rate %)'\n61\t    content: '| Expert      | Camera | Light | BG   | Noise | Layout | Robot | Avg   |\n62\t\n63\t      |-------------|--------|-------|------|-------|--------|-------|-------|\n64\t\n65\t      | LoRA-V      | **61.0** | **70.0** | **65.0** | **72.0** | 58.0 | 65.0 | 65.2\n66\t      |\n67\t\n68\t      | LoRA-S      | 53.0   | 63.0  | 57.0 | 65.0  | **66.0** | 67.0 | 61.8 |\n69\t\n70\t      | LoRA-P      | 54.0   | 64.0  | 58.0 | 66.0  | 60.0   | **73.0** | 62.5 |\n71\t\n72\t      | Universal   | 57.0   | 68.0  | 61.0 | 70.0  | 62.0   | 70.8  | 64.8 |\n73\t\n74\t      | Frozen pi0  | 44.0   | 56.0  | 49.0 | 58.0  | 52.0   | 62.6  | 53.6 |\n75\t\n76\t      Bold = expert matched to own perturbation type.\n77\t\n78\t      '\n79\t  - title: 'Specialization delta: Specialized − Universal per type'\n80\t    content: '| Type        | Specialized | Universal | Delta |\n81\t\n82\t      |-------------|-------------|-----------|-------|\n83\t\n84\t      | Camera      | 61.0        | 57.0      | +4.0  |\n85\t\n86\t      | Light       | 70.0        | 68.0      | +2.0  |\n87\t\n88\t      | Background  | 65.0        | 61.0      | +4.0  |\n89\t\n90\t      | Noise       | 72.0        | 70.0      | +2.0  |\n91\t\n92\t      | Layout      | 66.0        | 62.0      | +4.0  |\n93\t\n94\t      | Robot       | 73.0        | 70.8      | +2.2  |\n95\t\n96\t      | Max delta   |             |           | 4.0   |\n97\t\n98\t      '\n99\t  figures_description:\n100\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: per-perturbation success rate for each expert + universal.\n101\t    Shows experts are only slightly above universal on matched types and below on\n102\t    mismatched types.'\n103\t  - Heatmap of (Expert × Type) deltas relative to universal, highlighting the narrow\n104\t    specialization corridor.\n105\t  analysis: 'The cross-evaluation matrix reveals that rank-16 LoRA experts converge\n106\t    to similar correction strategies. Universal LoRA averages 64.8% overall, while\n107\t    matched experts average 67.8% on their own types — a difference of only 3.0%.\n108\t    Critically, mismatched experts often underperform universal (LoRA-V on layout:\n109\t    58.0 vs 62.0; LoRA-S on visual avg: 59.5 vs 64.0), meaning routing must be accurate\n110\t    just to avoid degradation. This creates a narrow \"routing benefit window\" where\n111\t    the expert-universal delta (~2–4%) must exceed the routing-error cost. At rank-16,\n112\t    the expert capacity bottleneck limits the maximum extractable benefit from routing,\n113\t    regardless of routing quality.\n114\t\n115\t    '\n116\t- name: Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank\n117\t  plan_id: plan_003\n118\t  description: 'Assemble PAR router (layer 6 MLP) with 3 LoRA experts under soft routing.\n119\t    End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters. Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus.\n120\t\n121\t    '\n122\t  expected_results:\n123\t    libero_plus_overall: 66.3\n124\t    gain_over_frozen: 12.7\n125\t    gain_over_universal: 1.5\n126\t    per_type_camera: 59.0\n127\t    per_type_light: 69.0\n128\t    per_type_background: 63.0\n129\t    per_type_noise: 71.0\n130\t    per_type_layout: 64.0\n131\t    per_type_robot: 72.0\n132\t    routing_diagonality: 0.58\n133\t    routing_weight_visual_on_visual: 0.52\n134\t    routing_weight_spatial_on_spatial: 0.61\n135\t    routing_weight_physical_on_physical: 0.63\n136\t    routing_entropy_mean: 0.89\n137\t  result_rationale: 'Soft routing weights are near-uniform (diagonality 0.58, mean\n138\t    entropy 0.89 vs max 1.10 for 3-class uniform). The PAR router correctly classifies\n139\t    perturbation type (Phase 0 F1=0.993) but the learned routing-to-correction mapping\n140\t    during fine-tuning converges toward distributing weight across all experts rather\n141\t    than concentrating on the matched one. This happens because the expert outputs\n142\t    are too similar (rank-16 convergence from plan_002) — the optimization landscape\n143\t    has no strong gradient toward diagonal routing since all experts provide nearly\n144\t    equal correction. The resulting Meta-Brain captures most of the universal LoRA\n145\t    gain (12.7 vs 11.2 over frozen) but adds only 1.5% from routing, which is within\n146\t    the noise floor of 5-seed evaluation.\n147\t\n148\t    '\n149\t  tables:\n150\t  - title: PAR routing weight matrix (rows = true type, columns = expert weight)\n151\t    content: '| True Type | w(LoRA-V) | w(LoRA-S) | w(LoRA-P) | Entropy |\n152\t\n153\t      |-----------|-----------|-----------|-----------|---------|\n154\t\n155\t      | Visual    | **0.52**  | 0.24      | 0.24      | 0.94    |\n156\t\n157\t      | Spatial   | 0.21      | **0.61**  | 0.18      | 0.83    |\n158\t\n159\t      | Physical  | 0.19      | 0.18      | **0.63**  | 0.80    |\n160\t\n161\t      | Diag avg  |           |           |           | 0.58    |\n162\t\n163\t      Perfect diagonality = 1.0, uniform = 0.33. Observed 0.58 = weak preference.\n164\t\n165\t      '\n166\t  - title: Meta-Brain per-perturbation results\n167\t    content: '| Perturbation | Frozen | Universal | Meta-Brain | Δ(MB−Uni) |\n168\t\n169\t      |--------------|--------|-----------|------------|-----------|\n170\t\n171\t      | Camera       | 44.0   | 57.0      | 59.0       | +2.0      |\n172\t\n173\t      | Light        | 56.0   | 68.0      | 69.0       | +1.0      |\n174\t\n175\t      | Background   | 49.0   | 61.0      | 63.0       | +2.0      |\n176\t\n177\t      | Noise        | 58.0   | 70.0      | 71.0       | +1.0      |\n178\t\n179\t      | Layout       | 52.0   | 62.0      | 64.0       | +2.0      |\n180\t\n181\t      | Robot        | 62.6   | 70.8      | 72.0       | +1.2      |\n182\t\n183\t      | **Overall**  | **53.6** | **64.8** | **66.3**   | **+1.5**  |\n184\t\n185\t      '\n186\t  figures_description:\n187\t  - 'Routing weight heatmap (3×3): near-uniform rather than diagonal, with max diagonal\n188\t    entry only 0.63.'\n189\t  - 'Per-perturbation stacked bar: Frozen → Universal gain (large) vs Universal →\n190\t    Meta-Brain gain (thin sliver) visually demonstrates marginal routing benefit.'\n191\t  analysis: 'Meta-Brain achieves 66.3% overall (12.7% gain over frozen) but only +1.5%\n192\t    over the universal LoRA baseline. The routing weight matrix is weakly diagonal\n193\t    (0.58) — the router assigns some preference to the matched expert but distributes\n194\t    substantial weight to others. This is not a routing failure (Phase 0 proves the\n195\t    signal exists) but a correction-capacity failure: when experts produce similar\n196\t    outputs, the optimization has no incentive to sharpen routing. The routing entropy\n197\t    (mean 0.89) is close to uniform (1.10), confirming near-flat routing. The 3x parameter\n198\t    overhead of maintaining separate experts (6M vs 2M) yields only 1.5% marginal\n199\t    gain, making the routing architecture difficult to justify on efficiency grounds\n200\t    alone.\n201\t\n202\t    '\n203\t- name: Oracle routing ablation\n204\t  plan_id: plan_004\n205\t  description: 'Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels. Measures\n206\t    upper bound of routing-based improvement.\n207\t\n208\t    '\n209\t  expected_results:\n210\t    oracle_overall: 67.8\n211\t    oracle_vs_par_gap: 1.5\n212\t    oracle_vs_universal_gap: 3.0\n213\t    oracle_camera: 61.0\n214\t    oracle_light: 70.0\n215\t    oracle_background: 65.0\n216\t    oracle_noise: 72.0\n217\t    oracle_layout: 66.0\n218\t    oracle_robot: 73.0\n219\t  result_rationale: 'Oracle routing achieves 67.8% — only 3.0% above universal LoRA\n220\t    and 1.5% above learned PAR routing. The small oracle-universal gap is the definitive\n221\t    evidence that the routing hypothesis is bottlenecked by expert differentiation,\n222\t    not routing quality. Even with perfect perturbation-type information, selecting\n223\t    the \"right\" rank-16 expert adds minimal value over the mixed-trained universal\n224\t    expert. The oracle-PAR gap of 1.5% indicates PAR routing recovers roughly half\n225\t    (1.5/3.0) of the theoretically available routing benefit, but the theoretical\n226\t    maximum itself is too small to matter. This ceiling analysis shifts the research\n227\t    question from \"how to route better\" to \"how to make experts more different.\"\n228\t\n229\t    '\n230\t  tables:\n231\t  - title: Oracle vs PAR vs Universal per type\n232\t    content: '| Type       | Universal | PAR(MB) | Oracle | Δ(Oracle−Uni) | Δ(PAR−Uni)\n233\t      |\n234\t\n235\t      |------------|-----------|---------|--------|---------------|------------|\n236\t\n237\t      | Camera     | 57.0      | 59.0    | 61.0   | +4.0          | +2.0       |\n238\t\n239\t      | Light      | 68.0      | 69.0    | 70.0   | +2.0          | +1.0       |\n240\t\n241\t      | Background | 61.0      | 63.0    | 65.0   | +4.0          | +2.0       |\n242\t\n243\t      | Noise      | 70.0      | 71.0    | 72.0   | +2.0          | +1.0       |\n244\t\n245\t      | Layout     | 62.0      | 64.0    | 66.0   | +4.0          | +2.0       |\n246\t\n247\t      | Robot      | 70.8      | 72.0    | 73.0   | +2.2          | +1.2       |\n248\t\n249\t      | **Overall**| **64.8**  |**66.3** |**67.8**| **+3.0**      | **+1.5**   |\n250\t\n251\t      '\n252\t  figures_description:\n253\t  - 'Gap waterfall chart: Universal → PAR (+1.5) → Oracle (+1.5) → showing the theoretical\n254\t    ceiling is low and PAR already captures half of it.'\n255\t  analysis: 'The oracle ablation is the most important diagnostic in the worst tier.\n256\t    Oracle routing = 67.8% sets an absolute ceiling for any routing-based approach\n257\t    with these rank-16 experts. The universal-to-oracle gap of 3.0% means perfect\n258\t    perturbation knowledge is worth at most 3 percentage points — a meaningful but\n259\t    modest gain that ranks below the universal LoRA''s own gain over frozen (11.2%).\n260\t    This proves the core routing hypothesis fails not because of routing errors but\n261\t    because of insufficient expert differentiation at rank 16.\n262\t\n263\t    '\n264\t- name: Ablation ladder — 5-seed systematic comparison\n265\t  plan_id: plan_005\n266\t  description: 'Core evidence table: Frozen → Universal LoRA → Meta-Brain → Oracle,\n267\t    evaluated on LIBERO-Plus with 5 seeds per condition. Statistical significance\n268\t    testing via paired t-test.\n269\t\n270\t    '\n271\t  expected_results:\n272\t    frozen_mean: 53.6\n273\t    frozen_std: 0.9\n274\t    universal_mean: 64.8\n275\t    universal_std: 1.1\n276\t    meta_brain_mean: 66.3\n277\t    meta_brain_std: 1.1\n278\t    oracle_mean: 67.8\n279\t    oracle_std: 1.0\n280\t    gain_frozen_to_universal: 11.2\n281\t    gain_frozen_to_meta_brain: 12.7\n282\t    gain_universal_to_meta_brain: 1.5\n283\t    gain_universal_to_meta_brain_std: 1.5\n284\t    gain_universal_to_meta_brain_pvalue: 0.12\n285\t    gain_universal_to_oracle: 3.0\n286\t    gain_universal_to_oracle_pvalue: 0.008\n287\t    gain_meta_brain_to_oracle: 1.5\n288\t  result_rationale: 'Five-seed evaluation reveals that Meta-Brain''s +1.5% gain over\n289\t    universal LoRA is not statistically significant (p=0.12, paired t-test). The seed-to-seed\n290\t    variance (std ~1.1%) is comparable to the routing-attributed gain, meaning the\n291\t    benefit could be noise. In contrast, universal LoRA''s +11.2% gain over frozen\n292\t    IS significant (p<0.001), confirming that LoRA adaptation itself works — the routing\n293\t    on top of it doesn''t. Oracle-universal gap (3.0%, p=0.008) is significant but\n294\t    small in absolute terms. The core story: most of the heavy lifting is done by\n295\t    LoRA adaptation (11.2/12.7 = 88% of total gain), with routing contributing at\n296\t    most 12% of the improvement, not statistically distinguishable from zero.\n297\t\n298\t    '\n299\t  tables:\n300\t  - title: 'Ablation ladder: Overall success rate (%, 5 seeds)'\n301\t    content: '| Method         | Params | Seed1 | Seed2 | Seed3 | Seed4 | Seed5 |\n302\t      Mean  | Std  |\n303\t\n304\t      |----------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|------|\n305\t\n306\t      | Frozen pi0     | 0      | 52.4  | 54.8  | 53.0  | 54.2  | 53.6  | 53.6  |\n307\t      0.9  |\n308\t\n309\t      | Universal LoRA | 2M     | 63.2  | 66.0  | 64.4  | 65.8  | 64.6  | 64.8  |\n310\t      1.1  |\n311\t\n312\t      | Meta-Brain     | 6M     | 65.0  | 67.4  | 66.2  | 67.0  | 65.9  | 66.3  |\n313\t      1.1  |\n314\t\n315\t      | Oracle routing | 6M     | 66.6  | 69.0  | 67.6  | 68.4  | 67.4  | 67.8  |\n316\t      1.0  |\n317\t\n318\t      '\n319\t  - title: Pairwise gains with significance\n320\t    content: '| Comparison                 | Δ Mean | Δ Std | p-value | Significant?\n321\t      |\n322\t\n323\t      |----------------------------|--------|-------|---------|-------------|\n324\t\n325\t      | Universal − Frozen         | +11.2  | 1.0   | <0.001  | Yes ***     |\n326\t\n327\t      | Meta-Brain − Frozen        | +12.7  | 1.0   | <0.001  | Yes ***     |\n328\t\n329\t      | Meta-Brain − Universal     | +1.5   | 1.5   | 0.12    | **No**      |\n330\t\n331\t      | Oracle − Universal         | +3.0   | 0.8   | 0.008   | Yes **      |\n332\t\n333\t      | Oracle − Meta-Brain        | +1.5   | 0.7   | 0.02    | Yes *       |\n334\t\n335\t      '\n336\t  - title: Per-perturbation breakdown (mean over 5 seeds)\n337\t    content: '| Type       | Frozen | Universal | Meta-Brain | Oracle | Δ(MB−Uni)\n338\t      |\n339\t\n340\t      |------------|--------|-----------|------------|--------|-----------|\n341\t\n342\t      | Camera     | 44.0   | 57.0      | 59.0       | 61.0   | +2.0      |\n343\t\n344\t      | Light      | 56.0   | 68.0      | 69.0       | 70.0   | +1.0      |\n345\t\n346\t      | Background | 49.0   | 61.0      | 63.0       | 65.0   | +2.0      |\n347\t\n348\t      | Noise      | 58.0   | 70.0      | 71.0       | 72.0   | +1.0      |\n349\t\n350\t      | Layout     | 52.0   | 62.0      | 64.0       | 66.0   | +2.0      |\n351\t\n352\t      | Robot      | 62.6   | 70.8      | 72.0       | 73.0   | +1.2      |\n353\t\n354\t      | **Overall**| **53.6**| **64.8** | **66.3**   |**67.8**| **+1.5**  |\n355\t\n356\t      '\n357\t  figures_description:\n358\t  - 'Ablation ladder bar chart with error bars (5 seeds): Frozen → Universal shows\n359\t    a large jump; Universal → Meta-Brain shows a thin, overlapping-CI sliver; Meta-Brain\n360\t    → Oracle shows another thin sliver. The visual immediately communicates that routing\n361\t    contributes negligible additional gain.'\n362\t  - 'Per-perturbation grouped bar chart (6 types × 4 methods): uniform small deltas\n363\t    across all types, no perturbation type shows dramatic routing benefit.'\n364\t  analysis: 'This is the central negative result. The ablation ladder shows a clear\n365\t    two-regime structure: (1) LoRA adaptation produces a large, significant gain (+11.2%),\n366\t    and (2) routing on top of LoRA produces a small, non-significant gain (+1.5%,\n367\t    p=0.12). The 95% CI for the Meta-Brain−Universal difference is [-0.5, +3.5], which\n368\t    includes zero. Even the oracle ceiling is only +3.0% above universal, meaning\n369\t    no routing strategy with these experts could achieve significance with 5 seeds.\n370\t    The per-perturbation breakdown shows remarkably uniform small deltas (+1.0 to\n371\t    +2.0), suggesting no perturbation type is a \"routing winner.\" This pattern is\n372\t    consistent with the hypothesis that rank-16 LoRA convergence, not routing quality,\n373\t    is the binding constraint.\n374\t\n375\t    '\n376\t- name: FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline\n377\t  plan_id: plan_006\n378\t  description: 'Reproduce FTM on pi0 for visual perturbation types. Compare with LoRA-V\n379\t    and Meta-Brain on visual perturbations. Establish Meta-Brain''s value proposition\n380\t    beyond visual-only methods.\n381\t\n382\t    '\n383\t  expected_results:\n384\t    ftm_camera: 63.0\n385\t    ftm_light: 71.0\n386\t    ftm_background: 62.0\n387\t    ftm_noise: 69.0\n388\t    ftm_visual_avg: 66.2\n389\t    lora_v_visual_avg: 67.0\n390\t    meta_brain_visual_avg: 65.5\n391\t    universal_visual_avg: 64.0\n392\t    ftm_on_layout: null\n393\t    ftm_on_robot: null\n394\t    meta_brain_overall_advantage: 1.5\n395\t  result_rationale: 'FTM achieves 66.2% on visual perturbations with only ~4K trainable\n396\t    parameters, which is competitive with LoRA-V (67.0%) and actually exceeds Meta-Brain''s\n397\t    visual performance (65.5%). FTM''s architecture (feature transformation modules)\n398\t    is specifically optimized for visual distribution shifts, giving it an edge on\n399\t    camera/light perturbations. Meta-Brain''s soft routing dilutes visual expert weight\n400\t    across all three experts (routing weight 0.52 for LoRA-V on visual inputs), so\n401\t    it underperforms the dedicated LoRA-V. Meta-Brain''s only advantage is coverage\n402\t    of spatial/physical types, but with the +1.5% overall gain over universal being\n403\t    non-significant, the coverage argument is weak. In worst tier, FTM''s parameter\n404\t    efficiency on visual perturbations (14.5% gain / 4K params) dramatically outperforms\n405\t    Meta-Brain''s overall efficiency.\n406\t\n407\t    '\n408\t  tables:\n409\t  - title: Visual perturbation comparison\n410\t    content: '| Method      | Params  | Camera | Light | BG   | Noise | Vis Avg |\n411\t\n412\t      |-------------|---------|--------|-------|------|-------|---------|\n413\t\n414\t      | Frozen pi0  | 0       | 44.0   | 56.0  | 49.0 | 58.0  | 51.8   |\n415\t\n416\t      | FTM         | 4K      | 63.0   | 71.0  | 62.0 | 69.0  | 66.2   |\n417\t\n418\t      | Universal   | 2M      | 57.0   | 68.0  | 61.0 | 70.0  | 64.0   |\n419\t\n420\t      | LoRA-V      | 2M      | 61.0   | 70.0  | 65.0 | 72.0  | 67.0   |\n421\t\n422\t      | Meta-Brain  | 6M      | 59.0   | 69.0  | 63.0 | 71.0  | 65.5   |\n423\t\n424\t      '\n425\t  - title: Coverage comparison\n426\t    content: '| Method     | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall | Coverage |\n427\t\n428\t      |------------|--------|---------|----------|---------|----------|\n429\t\n430\t      | FTM        | 66.2   | —       | —        | —       | Partial  |\n431\t\n432\t      | Meta-Brain | 65.5   | 64.0    | 72.0     | 66.3    | Full     |\n433\t\n434\t      | Universal  | 64.0   | 62.0    | 70.8     | 64.8    | Full     |\n435\t\n436\t      '\n437\t  figures_description:\n438\t  - 'Visual-perturbation-only bar chart: FTM vs LoRA-V vs Meta-Brain vs Universal.\n439\t    FTM is competitive or better than Meta-Brain on all visual types despite 1500x\n440\t    fewer parameters.'\n441\t  analysis: 'FTM comparison is damaging to Meta-Brain''s narrative in worst tier.\n442\t    FTM achieves comparable visual robustness with 4K params (66.2% visual avg) while\n443\t    Meta-Brain requires 6M params for 65.5% on visual types. Meta-Brain''s theoretical\n444\t    advantage — full perturbation coverage — yields only +1.5% overall gain over universal.\n445\t    A reviewer could argue: \"just use FTM for visual + universal LoRA for the rest\"\n446\t    as a simpler, more parameter-efficient alternative. In the pivot framing, this\n447\t    comparison supports the design-dimension analysis narrative: different adaptation\n448\t    strategies (feature transformation vs LoRA routing) have different efficiency\n449\t    profiles, and the optimal strategy depends on the perturbation distribution rather\n450\t    than routing quality.\n451\t\n452\t    '\n453\t- name: Parameter efficiency analysis\n454\t  plan_id: plan_007\n455\t  description: 'Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters.\n456\t    Scatter plot comparison across all methods including cross-paper baselines.\n457\t\n458\t    '\n459\t  expected_results:\n460\t    meta_brain_gain_over_frozen: 12.7\n461\t    meta_brain_params_M: 6.0\n462\t    meta_brain_gain_per_M: 2.12\n463\t    universal_gain_over_frozen: 11.2\n464\t    universal_params_M: 2.0\n465\t    universal_gain_per_M: 5.6\n466\t    ftm_gain_over_frozen_visual: 14.4\n467\t    ftm_params_M: 0.004\n468\t    ftm_gain_per_M_visual: 3600.0\n469\t    vla_gse_gain_over_lora: 12.0\n470\t    vla_gse_params_M: 114.0\n471\t    vla_gse_gain_per_M_over_lora: 0.105\n472\t    meta_brain_gain_over_lora: 1.5\n473\t    meta_brain_gain_per_M_over_lora: 0.25\n474\t  result_rationale: 'Parameter efficiency analysis is unfavorable for Meta-Brain in\n475\t    worst tier. Universal LoRA achieves 5.60% gain per million parameters — 2.6x more\n476\t    efficient than Meta-Brain (2.12). The 3x parameter budget (6M vs 2M) buys only\n477\t    1.5% additional gain. FTM dominates on visual-only efficiency (3600 gain/M on\n478\t    visual). When normalized to gain-over-LoRA-baseline (the routing-attributable\n479\t    improvement), Meta-Brain''s efficiency drops to 0.25%/M, meaning most of its gain\n480\t    comes from the LoRA components themselves, not the routing mechanism. The scatter\n481\t    plot will show Meta-Brain sitting in an unfavorable region: more parameters than\n482\t    universal LoRA for negligible additional gain, and far from FTM''s visual-only\n483\t    efficiency frontier.\n484\t\n485\t    '\n486\t  tables:\n487\t  - title: Parameter efficiency comparison\n488\t    content: '| Method         | Backbone   | Params  | Gain/Frozen | Gain/M  | Coverage\n489\t      |\n490\t\n491\t      |----------------|------------|---------|-------------|---------|----------|\n492\t\n493\t      | FTM            | pi0        | 4K      | +14.4 (vis) | 3600.0  | Visual   |\n494\t\n495\t      | Universal LoRA | pi0        | 2M      | +11.2       | 5.60    | Full     |\n496\t\n497\t      | Meta-Brain     | pi0        | 6M      | +12.7       | 2.12    | Full     |\n498\t\n499\t      | VLA-GSE*       | Qwen3-VL   | 114M    | +27.6†      | 0.24    | Full     |\n500\t\n501\t      * different backbone  † estimated gain over Qwen3-VL frozen baseline\n502\t\n503\t      '\n504\t  - title: Routing-attributable efficiency\n505\t    content: '| Method     | Gain over Uni LoRA | Routing params | Gain/M (routing)\n506\t      |\n507\t\n508\t      |------------|--------------------|----------------|------------------|\n509\t\n510\t      | Meta-Brain | +1.5               | 4M (extra)     | 0.375            |\n511\t\n512\t      | VLA-GSE*   | +12.0              | 114M           | 0.105            |\n513\t\n514\t      * cross-backbone, not directly comparable\n515\t\n516\t      '\n517\t  figures_description:\n518\t  - 'Log-scale scatter plot: x=trainable params, y=gain-over-frozen. Universal LoRA\n519\t    sits on a better efficiency frontier than Meta-Brain. FTM (visual-only) dominates\n520\t    the low-param region. Meta-Brain is right-shifted from Universal with minimal\n521\t    y-axis gain.'\n522\t  - 'Marginal efficiency bar chart: gain per additional M params spent beyond universal\n523\t    LoRA. Meta-Brain''s marginal efficiency (0.375%/M) is shown as negligible.'\n524\t  analysis: 'The parameter efficiency analysis is the quantitative nail in the routing\n525\t    hypothesis coffin. Universal LoRA achieves 88% of Meta-Brain''s total gain (11.2/12.7)\n526\t    with 33% of the parameters (2M/6M). The additional 4M parameters allocated to\n527\t    maintaining separate experts + router yield 0.375% gain per M — a poor return.\n528\t    In the pivot framing, this supports the design-dimension analysis: parameter-matched\n529\t    universal adaptation is a strong default, and routing-based approaches need either\n530\t    higher-capacity experts (rank 32+) or compositional perturbation scenarios to\n531\t    justify the overhead.\n532\t\n533\t    '\n534\t- name: Correction Memory ablation\n535\t  plan_id: plan_008\n536\t  description: 'Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM adds\n537\t    routing-weight modulation from retrieved exemplars via FAISS.\n538\t\n539\t    '\n540\t  expected_results:\n541\t    meta_brain_no_cm: 66.3\n542\t    meta_brain_with_cm: 66.7\n543\t    cm_ablation_delta: 0.4\n544\t    cm_delta_camera: 0.6\n545\t    cm_delta_light: 0.2\n546\t    cm_delta_background: 0.8\n547\t    cm_delta_noise: 0.0\n548\t    cm_delta_layout: 0.4\n549\t    cm_delta_robot: 0.2\n550\t    cm_pvalue: 0.38\n551\t  result_rationale: 'CM adds 0.4% overall — well within noise (p=0.38). Since the\n552\t    base routing weights are already near-uniform (diagonality 0.58), modulating them\n553\t    with retrieved exemplars has minimal effect: modulating a near-flat distribution\n554\t    stays near-flat. CM''s theoretical value is sharpening routing confidence for\n555\t    ambiguous cases, but when the experts themselves produce similar outputs, sharper\n556\t    routing does not translate to better correction. The per-type breakdown shows\n557\t    CM''s largest effect on background (+0.8%), which is the type with highest cross-expert\n558\t    variance in plan_002, but even this is not significant. CM adds one learned parameter\n559\t    (alpha) and CPU-bound FAISS retrieval — the complexity-to-gain ratio is extremely\n560\t    unfavorable.\n561\t\n562\t    '\n563\t  tables:\n564\t  - title: CM ablation per perturbation type\n565\t    content: '| Type       | No CM  | With CM | Δ    |\n566\t\n567\t      |------------|--------|---------|------|\n568\t\n569\t      | Camera     | 59.0   | 59.6    | +0.6 |\n570\t\n571\t      | Light      | 69.0   | 69.2    | +0.2 |\n572\t\n573\t      | Background | 63.0   | 63.8    | +0.8 |\n574\t\n575\t      | Noise      | 71.0   | 71.0    | +0.0 |\n576\t\n577\t      | Layout     | 64.0   | 64.4    | +0.4 |\n578\t\n579\t      | Robot      | 72.0   | 72.2    | +0.2 |\n580\t\n581\t      | **Overall**| **66.3**|**66.7**| **+0.4** |\n582\t\n583\t      p = 0.38 (paired t-test, not significant)\n584\t\n585\t      '\n586\t  figures_description:\n587\t  - 'Paired bar chart (with/without CM): differences are invisible at chart scale,\n588\t    visually reinforcing the null result.'\n589\t  analysis: 'CM is a clear null result in worst tier. Present as optional module with\n590\t    honest reporting: +0.4% (p=0.38). In the pivot framing, this supports the broader\n591\t    conclusion that the routing bottleneck is expert capacity, not routing precision.\n592\t    Even augmenting routing with episodic memory (retrieved exemplar context) cannot\n593\t    overcome the fundamental limitation that rank-16 experts produce similar outputs.\n594\t    Recommend presenting CM as a \"future work\" direction for higher-capacity expert\n595\t    settings rather than a contribution of this paper.\n596\t\n597\t    '\n598\t- name: Router architecture comparison\n599\t  plan_id: plan_009\n600\t  description: 'Compare PAR router variants: 2-layer MLP, linear classifier, attention-based\n601\t    router. All on PaliGemma layer 6. Measure routing accuracy and downstream task\n602\t    success.\n603\t\n604\t    '\n605\t  expected_results:\n606\t    mlp_routing_accuracy: 99.3\n607\t    mlp_downstream: 66.3\n608\t    linear_routing_accuracy: 98.1\n609\t    linear_downstream: 66.0\n610\t    attention_routing_accuracy: 99.1\n611\t    attention_downstream: 66.2\n612\t    downstream_range: 0.3\n613\t    routing_accuracy_range: 1.2\n614\t  result_rationale: 'All three router architectures produce nearly identical downstream\n615\t    performance (66.0–66.3%, range 0.3%) despite varying routing accuracy (98.1–99.3%).\n616\t    This confirms that routing quality is not the bottleneck: even the simplest linear\n617\t    classifier (98.1% accuracy) achieves 66.0%, only 0.3% below the best MLP router.\n618\t    The attention-based router, despite being more expressive, cannot extract additional\n619\t    routing value because the experts themselves are too similar. The routing accuracy\n620\t    → downstream transfer coefficient is near-zero: a 1.2% improvement in routing\n621\t    accuracy translates to at most 0.3% downstream gain. This dissociation between\n622\t    routing accuracy and downstream success is the strongest evidence that the correction-capacity\n623\t    bottleneck, not routing architecture, limits Meta-Brain''s effectiveness.\n624\t\n625\t    '\n626\t  tables:\n627\t  - title: Router architecture comparison\n628\t    content: '| Router     | Params (router) | Route Acc (%) | Downstream (%) | Δ\n629\t      from MLP |\n630\t\n631\t      |------------|-----------------|---------------|-----------------|------------|\n632\t\n633\t      | Linear     | 6K              | 98.1          | 66.0            | −0.3       |\n634\t\n635\t      | MLP (2-layer) | 33K          | 99.3          | 66.3            | —          |\n636\t\n637\t      | Attention  | 132K            | 99.1          | 66.2            | −0.1       |\n638\t\n639\t      '\n640\t  figures_description:\n641\t  - 'Dual-axis chart: routing accuracy (left y-axis) vs downstream success (right\n642\t    y-axis) for 3 router types. Routing accuracy varies more than downstream success,\n643\t    showing the disconnect.'\n644\t  analysis: 'Router architecture is irrelevant in worst tier. All variants perform\n645\t    within 0.3% of each other downstream, regardless of routing accuracy differences.\n646\t    This is useful for the design-dimension analysis: it provides evidence that perturbation-diagnostic\n647\t    routing is easy (even a linear classifier suffices) but that the downstream value\n648\t    of routing is limited by expert capacity. Recommend using the linear router in\n649\t    the paper (simplest, nearly identical performance) and framing the comparison\n650\t    as evidence for the \"routing is easy, correction is hard\" narrative.\n651\t\n652\t    '\n653\t- name: 6-class vs 3-class routing comparison\n654\t  plan_id: plan_010\n655\t  description: 'Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (6 LoRA experts, rank 8 each)\n656\t    vs 3-class coarse routing (3 LoRA experts, rank 16 each) under matched total parameter\n657\t    budget (~6M).\n658\t\n659\t    '\n660\t  expected_results:\n661\t    three_class_overall: 66.3\n662\t    six_class_overall: 65.8\n663\t    six_class_delta: -0.5\n664\t    six_class_camera: 59.4\n665\t    six_class_light: 69.2\n666\t    six_class_background: 62.4\n667\t    six_class_noise: 70.0\n668\t    six_class_layout: 63.6\n669\t    six_class_robot: 70.2\n670\t    six_class_routing_accuracy: 98.8\n671\t    six_class_routing_diagonality: 0.51\n672\t    marginal_gain_per_expert: -0.08\n673\t  result_rationale: '6-class routing performs slightly worse than 3-class (65.8 vs\n674\t    66.3, Δ=−0.5%). Under a fixed parameter budget, doubling the number of experts\n675\t    halves each expert''s rank (rank 8 vs rank 16), reducing individual expert capacity.\n676\t    The finer routing granularity cannot compensate for the reduced per-expert correction\n677\t    power. Additionally, 6-class routing diagonality (0.51) is lower than 3-class\n678\t    (0.58) — with more classes, the routing weights spread more thinly, approaching\n679\t    uniform distribution. The intra-Visual perturbations (camera, light, background,\n680\t    noise) occupy overlapping representation spaces in PaliGemma layer 6 (consistent\n681\t    with Phase 0''s 6-class accuracy being only marginally above 3-class), so splitting\n682\t    them into separate experts creates artificial boundaries in a continuous representation\n683\t    space. This result validates the 3-class taxonomy and argues against finer granularity\n684\t    at this parameter budget.\n685\t\n686\t    '\n687\t  tables:\n688\t  - title: 6-class vs 3-class comparison\n689\t    content: '| Config  | Experts | Rank/Expert | Total Params | Overall | Diag  |\n690\t\n691\t      |---------|---------|-------------|-------------|---------|-------|\n692\t\n693\t      | 3-class | 3       | 16          | ~6M         | 66.3    | 0.58  |\n694\t\n695\t      | 6-class | 6       | 8           | ~6M         | 65.8    | 0.51  |\n696\t\n697\t      | Δ       |         |             |             | −0.5    | −0.07 |\n698\t\n699\t      '\n700\t  - title: 6-class per-perturbation results\n701\t    content: '| Type       | 3-class | 6-class | Δ    |\n702\t\n703\t      |------------|---------|---------|------|\n704\t\n705\t      | Camera     | 59.0    | 59.4    | +0.4 |\n706\t\n707\t      | Light      | 69.0    | 69.2    | +0.2 |\n708\t\n709\t      | Background | 63.0    | 62.4    | −0.6 |\n710\t\n711\t      | Noise      | 71.0    | 70.0    | −1.0 |\n712\t\n713\t      | Layout     | 64.0    | 63.6    | −0.4 |\n714\t\n715\t      | Robot      | 72.0    | 70.2    | −1.8 |\n716\t\n717\t      '\n718\t  figures_description:\n719\t  - 'Side-by-side routing heatmaps: 3×3 (3-class) vs 6×6 (6-class). 6-class shows\n720\t    more diffuse routing weights with weaker diagonal.'\n721\t  analysis: 'Finer granularity hurts under fixed parameter budget. Camera and light\n722\t    benefit marginally (+0.4, +0.2) from dedicated experts, but robot (−1.8) and noise\n723\t    (−1.0) degrade substantially — their rank-8 experts lack capacity for effective\n724\t    correction. This is a capacity-granularity tradeoff: more routing precision ×\n725\t    less correction capacity = net negative. Addresses reviewer concern C5 directly:\n726\t    3-class is not too coarse; it is actually the better operating point given the\n727\t    rank-16 constraint. Finer granularity would require proportionally higher total\n728\t    rank budget.\n729\t\n730\t    '\n731\t- name: Cross-backbone validation — SmolVLA\n732\t  plan_id: plan_011\n733\t  description: 'Replicate Phase 0 + Phase 1 core pipeline on SmolVLA to test generalizability\n734\t    of perturbation-diagnostic routing across architectures.\n735\t\n736\t    '\n737\t  expected_results:\n738\t    smolvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.971\n739\t    smolvla_phase0_best_layer: 4\n740\t    smolvla_phase0_k1_f1: 0.943\n741\t    smolvla_frozen_baseline: 48.2\n742\t    smolvla_universal_lora: 58.6\n743\t    smolvla_meta_brain: 59.8\n744\t    smolvla_gain_over_frozen: 11.6\n745\t    smolvla_gain_over_universal: 1.2\n746\t    smolvla_routing_diagonality: 0.54\n747\t  result_rationale: 'Phase 0 replicates strongly on SmolVLA (3-class F1=0.971, K=1\n748\t    F1=0.943) — perturbation type is diagnosable from frozen activations across architectures.\n749\t    This is the central positive finding for the pivot narrative: VLA internal representations\n750\t    encode perturbation type as a general property, not a pi0 artifact. Phase 1 shows\n751\t    the same pattern as pi0: large universal LoRA gain (+10.4 over frozen), marginal\n752\t    routing gain (+1.2 over universal, not significant). SmolVLA''s lower absolute\n753\t    performance (48.2% frozen, 59.8% Meta-Brain) reflects its smaller backbone capacity,\n754\t    but the relative pattern is identical — LoRA adaptation helps, routing doesn''t\n755\t    add much. Routing diagonality (0.54) is even weaker than pi0 (0.58), consistent\n756\t    with SmolVLA''s slightly lower Phase 0 accuracy. Cross-backbone replication strengthens\n757\t    Phase 0 as the lead contribution and confirms that the routing limitation is a\n758\t    property of the rank-16 LoRA approach, not a backbone-specific artifact.\n759\t\n760\t    '\n761\t  tables:\n762\t  - title: Cross-backbone Phase 0 comparison\n763\t    content: '| Metric            | pi0 (PaliGemma) | SmolVLA      |\n764\t\n765\t      |-------------------|-----------------|--------------|\n766\t\n767\t      | 3-class F1        | 0.993           | 0.971        |\n768\t\n769\t      | Best layer        | 6               | 4            |\n770\t\n771\t      | K=1 F1            | 1.000           | 0.943        |\n772\t\n773\t      | Binary AUC        | 1.000           | 0.998        |\n774\t\n775\t      '\n776\t  - title: Cross-backbone Phase 1 ablation\n777\t    content: '| Method         | pi0   | SmolVLA | Pattern match? |\n778\t\n779\t      |----------------|-------|---------|----------------|\n780\t\n781\t      | Frozen         | 53.6  | 48.2    | —              |\n782\t\n783\t      | Universal LoRA | 64.8  | 58.6    | —              |\n784\t\n785\t      | Meta-Brain     | 66.3  | 59.8    | —              |\n786\t\n787\t      | Δ(MB−Uni)      | +1.5  | +1.2    | ✓ same pattern |\n788\t\n789\t      | Routing diag   | 0.58  | 0.54    | ✓ same pattern |\n790\t\n791\t      '\n792\t  figures_description:\n793\t  - 't-SNE comparison: pi0 layer 6 vs SmolVLA layer 4 activations, both showing clear\n794\t    3-class clustering. Visual evidence that perturbation diagnosability is architecture-general.'\n795\t  - 'Mirror ablation ladder: pi0 (left) vs SmolVLA (right) with matching relative\n796\t    patterns despite different absolute baselines.'\n797\t  analysis: 'Cross-backbone replication is the strongest evidence for the pivot paper.\n798\t    Phase 0 generalizes (F1>0.97 on SmolVLA) while Phase 1''s routing limitation also\n799\t    generalizes (Δ=+1.2, not significant). This supports the reframed contribution:\n800\t    \"VLA representations universally encode perturbation type\" (positive finding,\n801\t    lead contribution) + \"diagnostic routing yields marginal downstream gains at rank-16\"\n802\t    (negative-but-informative, generalizes across backbones). SmolVLA''s different\n803\t    architecture (different vision encoder, different layer structure) makes the Phase\n804\t    0 replication a genuine generalization result, not a trivial confirmation. Addresses\n805\t    reviewer concern C3 directly.\n806\t\n807\t    '\n808\t- name: LoRA layer placement ablation\n809\t  plan_id: plan_012\n810\t  description: 'Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: early (2-6), mid (6-12),\n811\t    late (12-16), all layers. Test whether type-specific layer placement improves\n812\t    specialization.\n813\t\n814\t    '\n815\t  expected_results:\n816\t    early_layers_overall: 65.8\n817\t    mid_layers_overall: 66.1\n818\t    late_layers_overall: 65.4\n819\t    all_layers_overall: 66.5\n820\t    early_visual_advantage: 0.6\n821\t    mid_spatial_advantage: 0.4\n822\t    late_physical_advantage: 0.2\n823\t    optimal_vs_default_gap: 0.2\n824\t    visual_lora_best_placement: early (2-6)\n825\t    spatial_lora_best_placement: mid (6-12)\n826\t    physical_lora_best_placement: all\n827\t  result_rationale: 'Layer placement produces negligible differences (65.4–66.5%,\n828\t    range 1.1%). The hypothesis that Visual LoRA benefits from early layers, Spatial\n829\t    from mid, and Physical from late receives only weak support: Visual expert on\n830\t    early layers gains +0.6% on visual types (compared to default all-layer), Spatial\n831\t    on mid gains +0.4% on layout, but Physical on late layers actually degrades (−0.8%\n832\t    on robot). The \"all layers\" placement (66.5%) is marginally best overall — spreading\n833\t    LoRA across all layers provides the most general correction. At rank 16, the LoRA\n834\t    correction is too low-rank to benefit from layer-specific placement: the correction\n835\t    capacity is distributed thinly regardless of which layers are targeted. The type-specific\n836\t    placement hypothesis would require higher-rank experts where layer-specific corrections\n837\t    could diverge more substantially. This ablation provides mild evidence for the\n838\t    \"experts converge to similar solutions\" hypothesis — even varying their layer\n839\t    targets doesn''t produce differentiated behaviors.\n840\t\n841\t    '\n842\t  tables:\n843\t  - title: Layer placement overall results\n844\t    content: '| Placement    | Layers  | Overall | Visual | Spatial | Physical |\n845\t\n846\t      |--------------|---------|---------|--------|---------|----------|\n847\t\n848\t      | Early        | 2-6     | 65.8    | 66.1   | 63.0    | 71.0     |\n849\t\n850\t      | Mid          | 6-12    | 66.1    | 65.4   | 64.8    | 72.2     |\n851\t\n852\t      | Late         | 12-16   | 65.4    | 64.8   | 63.2    | 71.8     |\n853\t\n854\t      | All (default)| 2-16    | 66.5    | 65.5   | 64.0    | 72.0     |\n855\t\n856\t      | Range        |         | 1.1     | 1.3    | 1.8     | 1.2      |\n857\t\n858\t      '\n859\t  - title: Type-specific placement hypothesis test\n860\t    content: '| Expert  | Hypothesis        | Best placement | Δ vs all-layers | Confirmed?\n861\t      |\n862\t\n863\t      |---------|-------------------|----------------|-----------------|------------|\n864\t\n865\t      | LoRA-V  | Early best        | Early (+0.6)   | +0.6            | Weak       |\n866\t\n867\t      | LoRA-S  | Mid best          | Mid (+0.8)     | +0.8            | Weak       |\n868\t\n869\t      | LoRA-P  | Late best         | All (−0.8 late)| −0.8            | No         |\n870\t\n871\t      '\n872\t  figures_description:\n873\t  - 'Heatmap: (layer placement × perturbation type) success rates. Shows flat landscape\n874\t    with no strong type-layer interaction.'\n875\t  analysis: 'Layer placement is a near-null result. The 1.1% overall range is within\n876\t    seed-level variance, and no type-layer pairing produces a compelling advantage.\n877\t    Physical expert on late layers is actually worse, contradicting the mechanistic\n878\t    hypothesis. At rank 16, the LoRA updates are too constrained to develop layer-specific\n879\t    correction strategies. This supports the overall worst-tier narrative: the rank-16\n880\t    LoRA bottleneck prevents meaningful specialization regardless of routing quality,\n881\t    expert assignment, or layer targeting. Present as supporting evidence for the\n882\t    \"capacity is the bottleneck\" conclusion and a clear direction for future work\n883\t    (higher-rank experts + layer-aware placement).'\n884\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LQ4Te7gZVbHZuRYt6tuXWZ","session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"b9d1c474-fe39-4f29-aee8-302447dbcf4a","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:39:09.687Z"}
