{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Us9UjeDe9QPXkuMj2Uymnn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic LoRA Routing for\\\\Robust Vision-Language-Action Models}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{\n16\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n17\t  CoRL 2026 Submission\n18\t}\n19\t\n20\t\\date{}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t%% ============================================================\n26\t%%  ABSTRACT\n27\t%% ============================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) achieve high success rates on standard manipulation benchmarks but degrade sharply under environment perturbations.\n30\tExisting robustness methods either apply perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation or require large parameter budgets for capacity-based expert routing.\n31\tWe observe that frozen VLA internal activations already encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (3-class macro-F1 $\\geq 0.99$ at a single layer), yet no existing method exploits this diagnostic signal for targeted correction.\n32\tWe propose \\textbf{Meta-Brain}, a parameter-efficient framework that closes the diagnose--correct loop: a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) classifies the active perturbation type from a single frozen hidden state, then soft-routes corrective signals through a bank of type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual / Spatial / Physical).\n33\tOn LIBERO-Plus with a pi0 backbone, Meta-Brain achieves 71.4\\% overall success with ${\\sim}6$M trainable parameters---an 17.8\\% improvement over the frozen baseline and 6.0\\% over a parameter-matched universal LoRA---capturing 64.5\\% of VLA-GSE's absolute robustness gain at 5.3\\% of its parameter cost.\n34\tCross-backbone validation on SmolVLA replicates the qualitative pattern (+5.6\\% over universal), confirming that perturbation-diagnostic routing is an architecture-independent principle.\n35\tA mechanistic analysis reveals that routing benefit is strongest for perturbation types that disrupt localized processing stages (visual, physical) and weakest for those requiring trajectory-level replanning (spatial), providing actionable design guidance for future robustness methods.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t%% ============================================================\n39\t%%  1. INTRODUCTION\n40\t%% ============================================================\n41\t\\section{Introduction}\n42\t\n43\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models have achieved remarkable performance on standard manipulation benchmarks, with policies such as pi0 exceeding 90\\% success on LIBERO.\n44\tHowever, these models remain brittle when deployed under environment perturbations: on the recently introduced LIBERO-Plus benchmark, which evaluates robustness across seven perturbation types (camera, lighting, background, noise, distractor objects, layout changes, and robot dynamics), frozen pi0 achieves only 53.6\\%.\n45\t\n46\tExisting approaches to VLA robustness fall into several categories.\n47\t\\emph{Capacity-based routing} methods such as VLA-GSE~\\cite{vla-gse} add Mixture-of-Expert layers that route by per-token capacity needs, achieving 81.2\\% on LIBERO-Plus but requiring 114M trainable parameters.\n48\t\\emph{Visual-only correction} methods such as FTM and FLA achieve strong results on visual perturbations with minimal parameters (4K--4.7M) but cannot address spatial or physical perturbations.\n49\t\\emph{Uniform adaptation} approaches, including adversarial training (RobustVLA) and universal LoRA, apply the same correction regardless of perturbation type.\n50\tNone of these methods exploit the fact that different perturbation types disrupt different VLA processing stages---visual perturbations corrupt early representations, spatial perturbations invalidate action trajectories, and physical perturbations break dynamics assumptions---and thus demand qualitatively different corrections.\n51\t\n52\tWe make three observations that motivate our approach.\n53\tFirst, frozen VLA activations at a single intermediate layer (PaliGemma layer 6 in pi0) encode perturbation type with near-perfect discriminability (3-class macro-F1 = 0.993), enabling zero-overhead real-time diagnosis from a single forward pass.\n54\tSecond, type-specialized LoRA experts substantially outperform universal LoRA on their target perturbation type (e.g., +13.0\\% for Visual, +9.0\\% for Physical) but \\emph{degrade} performance when applied to the wrong type, making accurate routing critical.\n55\tThird, the optimal layer placement for LoRA correction differs by perturbation type---early layers for visual, mid layers for spatial, late layers for physical---confirming that perturbation types disrupt distinct processing stages.\n56\t\n57\tBased on these observations, we propose \\textbf{Meta-Brain}, a modular framework consisting of:\n58\t(1)~a \\emph{Perturbation-Aware Router} (PAR)---a lightweight MLP classifier (${\\sim}50$K params) operating on frozen layer-6 activations;\n59\t(2)~an \\emph{Adaptive LoRA Bank} (ALB)---three rank-16 LoRA adapters specialized for Visual, Spatial, and Physical perturbations (${\\sim}6$M params total);\n60\tand (3)~an optional \\emph{Correction Memory} (CM) module that modulates routing weights using retrieved exemplars.\n61\tThe PAR produces soft routing weights that blend expert corrections: $\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{corrected}} = \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}} + \\sum_k p(\\text{type}_k) \\cdot \\text{LoRA}_k(\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{frozen}})$.\n62\t\n63\tOur contributions are:\n64\t\\begin{enumerate}\n65\t\\item \\textbf{Empirical finding}: Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity, enabling real-time diagnosis without external modules.\n66\t\\item \\textbf{Architecture}: Meta-Brain---a parameter-efficient framework that routes corrective LoRA experts by diagnosed perturbation type, achieving 71.4\\% on LIBERO-Plus with ${\\sim}6$M parameters.\n67\t\\item \\textbf{Design dimension analysis}: Systematic comparison establishing perturbation-diagnostic routing as an orthogonal and composable axis relative to capacity-based routing, universal adaptation, and visual-only correction.\n68\t\\end{enumerate}\n69\t\n70\t%% ============================================================\n71\t%%  2. RELATED WORK\n72\t%% ============================================================\n73\t\\section{Related Work}\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{VLA Robustness.}\n76\tLIBERO-Plus introduced a standardized robustness evaluation covering seven perturbation types.\n77\tVLA-GSE adds Mixture-of-Expert layers for capacity routing (114M params, 81.2\\%).\n78\tRobustVLA applies adversarial training for uniform robustness.\n79\tSRPO and CorridorVLA achieve 82.1\\% and 83.2\\% via full-model training.\n80\tThese methods are perturbation-agnostic: the same model configuration handles all perturbation types identically.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Visual Perturbation Correction.}\n83\tFTM corrects visual perturbations with 4K parameters via fine-tuned visual transforms.\n84\tFLA extends this with learned attention-based correction (4.7M params).\n85\tBoth are limited to visual perturbation types and cannot address spatial or physical perturbations.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Parameter-Efficient Adaptation for VLAs.}\n88\tLoRA and its variants (LoRA-SP, QLoRA) enable efficient fine-tuning.\n89\tTask-specific routing (MoIRA, CORAL) selects adapters by task identity, not perturbation type.\n90\tMeta-Brain introduces perturbation-type routing as a new routing dimension orthogonal to task routing.\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Perturbation Diagnosis in VLAs.}\n93\tSAFE demonstrates that VLA internal features can detect out-of-distribution inputs.\n94\tProbing studies on OpenVLA show linear separability of environment factors.\n95\tFeature steering on pi0.5 demonstrates causal influence of internal representations.\n96\tMeta-Brain is the first to close the loop from diagnosis to correction.\n97\t\n98\t%% ============================================================\n99\t%%  3. METHOD\n100\t%% ============================================================\n101\t\\section{Method}\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n104\t\n105\tConsider a frozen VLA policy $\\pi_\\theta$ that maps observations $\\mathbf{o}_t$ to actions $\\mathbf{a}_t$.\n106\tUnder a perturbation of type $k \\in \\{\\text{Visual}, \\text{Spatial}, \\text{Physical}\\}$, performance degrades from baseline.\n107\tOur goal is to restore performance via a lightweight, modular adaptation that is aware of the active perturbation type.\n108\t\n109\t\\subsection{Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR)}\n110\t\n111\tThe PAR operates on frozen hidden states $\\mathbf{h}^{(\\ell)}$ at layer $\\ell$ of the VLA's vision encoder.\n112\tWe select layer $\\ell=6$ of PaliGemma (pi0's vision backbone) based on probing analysis showing peak perturbation discriminability at this layer (3-class macro-F1 = 0.993).\n113\t\n114\tThe router is a 2-layer MLP with ${\\sim}50$K parameters:\n115\t\\begin{equation}\n116\t  p(k \\mid \\mathbf{o}_t) = \\text{softmax}\\!\\bigl(\\text{MLP}(\\bar{\\mathbf{h}}^{(6)}_t)\\bigr), \\quad k \\in \\{V, S, P\\}\n117\t\\end{equation}\n118\twhere $\\bar{\\mathbf{h}}^{(6)}_t$ is the mean-pooled hidden state across spatial tokens at layer 6.\n119\tPAR is initialized from probing weights (Phase~0) and end-to-end fine-tuned for task success.\n120\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SCpZUDUWJBkc4eqtEYBXAg","session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"84ad1122-aa6f-4f91-858f-a64f7e463b46","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:39:43.669Z"}
