{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a2d7d90eefab01575","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LQ4Te7gZVbHZuRYt6tuXWZ","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","usage":{"total_tokens":131923,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":129460},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"0f609bdf-317a-449f-80be-71a96a63a420","session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0181MFMFDG3RaBNuMvTYnHUM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V8BYsysqTogLGDNmeVoPAu","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"meta_brain_lora_routing","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260515T154653","overall_score":"borderline accept","report":"# Reviewer R1 — Methods & Experiments Focus\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nMeta-Brain proposes perturbation-diagnostic LoRA routing for robust VLAs: a lightweight MLP router (PAR) reads a single layer of frozen pi0 activations, classifies the perturbation type (Visual/Spatial/Physical), and soft-routes to one of three specialized rank-16 LoRA experts. On LIBERO-Plus the system achieves 71.4% overall (vs. 53.6% frozen, 65.4% universal LoRA, 75.3% oracle) with ~6M trainable parameters. The paper makes three claims: frozen activations encode perturbation type (Phase 0), targeted routing outperforms uniform LoRA (Phase 1), and the approach is parameter-efficient relative to VLA-GSE (114M params).\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Clean ablation ladder with statistical rigor.** The Frozen (53.6) < Universal (65.4) < Meta-Brain (71.4) < Oracle (75.3) ordering holds across all perturbation types with 5-seed evaluation. All adjacent pairs are significant at p < 0.05. The inclusion of random routing as an implicit control (not explicitly reported but implied by the cross-type degradation data) would strengthen this further.\n\n- **Phase 0 finding is genuinely compelling.** Single-layer, single-step, single-token classification with F1 = 0.993 (and K=1 F1 = 1.000 with first-token pooling) is a strong empirical finding. The progression from episode-level to per-step to K=1 analysis is methodologically sound and carefully documented. This is arguably the paper's strongest contribution.\n\n- **Comprehensive experiment coverage.** The paper covers 11 experiments spanning expert specialization, assembly, oracle upper bound, ablation ladder, FTM comparison, parameter efficiency, CM ablation, router architecture, 6-class vs 3-class, cross-backbone, and layer placement. This breadth is unusual and appreciated.\n\n- **Cross-backbone validation on SmolVLA.** Comparable routing gains (+5.6% over universal, vs. +6.0% on pi0) on a different architecture (SigLIP + SmolLM) substantially strengthen the generalizability claim. The consistent spatial underperformance across backbones is also informative.\n\n- **Honest parameter accounting.** The paper correctly notes that Meta-Brain uses 3x the parameters of universal LoRA (6M vs 2M), and frames the comparison as routing benefit rather than just parameter scaling. The rank-48 universal LoRA control (predicted ~67%) would be a valuable addition to disambiguate these effects.\n\n- **Expert cross-evaluation matrix.** The strong diagonal structure (specialists +9 to +13% over universal on own type, below frozen on mismatched types) directly motivates routing as a necessity, not just an optimization. The asymmetric degradation data is convincing.\n\n- **Layer placement ablation provides mechanistic depth.** The V-early, S-mid, P-late crossed peak pattern aligns with the proposed processing-stage hypothesis and adds interpretability beyond mere performance numbers.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Spatial routing gain is negligible (+1.1% over universal) and captures only 25.6% of the oracle ceiling.** The paper claims Meta-Brain handles all three perturbation categories, but the spatial results are barely above the universal baseline. The routing diagonality for spatial (0.68) is substantially lower than visual (0.82) or physical (0.80). For 2 of the 7 perturbation types (distractor +0.4%, layout +1.8%), Meta-Brain provides essentially no routing benefit. The mechanistic explanation (trajectory-level replanning beyond representation correction) is plausible but raises a deeper question: if LoRA cannot correct spatial perturbations at the representation level, then calling the 3-class taxonomy \"Visual/Spatial/Physical\" overpromises on the spatial dimension. The Spatial expert is not actually correcting spatial perturbations — it is doing something marginal at best.\n\n- **Parameter-unfair comparison: Meta-Brain (6M) vs. Universal LoRA (2M).** The headline comparison should be against a parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank-48, ~6M params). The current ablation ladder compares 6M routed vs. 2M universal, conflating routing benefit with raw parameter increase. The paper acknowledges this (\"rank-48 universal LoRA predicted ~67%\") but this critical control is not actually run. Without it, a skeptical reader cannot distinguish \"routing helps\" from \"3x more parameters help.\" This is the most important missing experiment.\n\n- **VLA-GSE comparison is cross-backbone and therefore weak.** Comparing gain-over-frozen across different backbones (pi0 vs. Qwen3-VL-4B) is informative but not rigorous. The frozen baselines are coincidentally similar (53.6% for both), which makes the comparison look fairer than it is — the backbone capacities, training data, and architectural properties differ substantially. The \"64.5% of VLA-GSE's gain at 5.3% of parameters\" framing is misleading without extensive caveats.\n\n- **Oracle-PAR gap of 3.9% is non-trivial.** Meta-Brain captures only 60.6% of the theoretically available routing benefit (6.0% out of 9.9% oracle-universal gap). Framing this as \"PAR captures most of the routing benefit\" is optimistic — 40% left on the table is substantial. The gap is statistically significant (p = 0.021), meaning the learned router meaningfully underperforms perfect routing.\n\n- **The 3-class taxonomy conflates observation about LIBERO-Plus with a general design principle.** The taxonomy is derived from the specific perturbation types available in LIBERO-Plus, not from any principled analysis of perturbation spaces in general robotics. The paper presents it as a mechanistic insight (early/mid/late processing stages), but the mapping from perturbation types to processing stages is post-hoc and not causally validated. The layer placement ablation supports the hypothesis but doesn't rule out alternative explanations.\n\n- **Correction Memory is padding, not a contribution.** CM adds +1.2% overall with p-values not reported for this specific comparison. With only 1 learned parameter (alpha = 0.23), it is difficult to argue this constitutes a meaningful architectural contribution. The FAISS infrastructure adds deployment complexity for negligible gain. The paper would be stronger without CM in the main method, presenting it only as a supplementary ablation.\n\n- **Training data asymmetry across experts is concerning.** LoRA-V uses online visual augmentation of standard demonstrations (action labels unchanged), while LoRA-S and LoRA-P require collecting new expert demonstrations in perturbed environments (~200 per category). This asymmetry means expert quality differences could reflect data quality/quantity rather than architectural suitability. The universal LoRA's training data composition (mix of all sources) is also underspecified.\n\n- **\"Frozen activations encode perturbation type\" is less novel/surprising than presented.** SAFE (NeurIPS 2025) already showed that VLA internal representations encode failure modes with ROC-AUC = 0.78 for binary detection on OpenVLA. The jump from binary anomaly detection to 3-class perturbation classification is meaningful but incremental. Feature steering papers (pi0.5, OpenVLA) have also demonstrated that VLA representations are highly structured. The Phase 0 result confirms a known property more precisely rather than discovering something fundamentally new.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Parameter-matched control:** What is the performance of a rank-48 universal LoRA (~6M params, matching Meta-Brain's total parameter budget)? This is the single most critical missing comparison for Claim 2.\n\n2. **Random routing baseline:** The ablation ladder in the paper summary mentions \"random routing\" was planned (plan_005) but only Frozen/Universal/Meta-Brain/Oracle are reported. What happened to the random routing control? If random routing with 3 experts achieves ~67-68% (somewhere between universal and Meta-Brain), the routing benefit shrinks considerably.\n\n3. **Spatial underperformance:** Given that Meta-Brain provides essentially no routing benefit on spatial perturbations (distractor +0.4%, layout +1.8%), would a 2-class system (Visual + Non-Visual) perform comparably with fewer parameters (2 experts instead of 3)?\n\n4. **Training-deployment feature mismatch:** Phase 0 classifier was trained on episode-averaged activations (~44 steps) but the deployed router uses single-step features (K=1). The K=1 validation bridges this gap for classification, but does the end-to-end routing fine-tuning use single-step features? Clarify the exact feature extraction procedure during Meta-Brain inference.\n\n5. **Soft vs. hard routing:** Oracle routing uses hard (1-hot) assignment and achieves 75.3%. PAR uses soft routing and achieves 71.4%. Is the 3.9% gap attributable to soft-routing dilution or to routing errors? An ablation with hard PAR routing (argmax of PAR predictions) would disambiguate.\n\n6. **Perturbation detection at deployment:** The system assumes the presence of a perturbation and routes accordingly. What happens on clean (unperturbed) inputs? Does the router default to a specific expert, or does the system need an additional clean/perturbed binary classifier?\n\n7. **Why not joint training?** The current pipeline trains experts independently then freezes them while fine-tuning only the router. Has joint training of router + experts been attempted? The frozen-expert constraint seems to limit system performance unnecessarily.\n\n## 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **Parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank-48 or rank-32+16).** Essential for a fair Claim 2 comparison. Without this, the routing benefit cannot be cleanly separated from the parameter scaling benefit. This is a blocking concern.\n\n2. **Random routing baseline.** Planned (plan_005) but not reported. Needed to rule out ensemble effects — if randomly selecting one of 3 specialized experts per episode performs similarly to Meta-Brain, then routing is irrelevant.\n\n3. **Confidence intervals and per-seed breakdowns for key comparisons.** The paper reports mean +/- std but the Meta-Brain vs. Universal comparison (6.0 +/- 2.3%, p = 0.008) has wide confidence intervals relative to the effect size. Paired per-seed comparisons would strengthen this.\n\n4. **Clean/unperturbed evaluation.** How does Meta-Brain perform on standard LIBERO (no perturbation)? If routing adds overhead that degrades clean performance, the method has limited practical value.\n\n5. **Rank sensitivity analysis.** The entire negative case for routing (worst tier) hinges on rank-16 being insufficient for expert differentiation. A rank-32 or rank-64 comparison would clarify whether expert capacity or routing quality is the binding constraint.\n\n6. **Compositional perturbations.** All LIBERO-Plus perturbations are single-type. Real-world scenarios involve simultaneous perturbations (e.g., camera change + object rearrangement). How does the router handle multi-label inputs, and does soft routing provide any advantage over hard routing in this regime?\n\n7. **Wall-clock latency analysis.** The paper claims zero-overhead routing from a single forward pass, but does not report actual inference latency comparisons between frozen pi0, universal LoRA, and Meta-Brain.\n\n## 6. Scores\n\n- **Soundness (1-4): 3.** The method is technically correct but the parameter-unfair comparison weakens Claim 2. The spatial routing failure is acknowledged but not adequately addressed.\n\n- **Empirical Rigor (1-4): 3.** Impressive experiment breadth (11 experiments) with 5-seed evaluation and p-values. Missing the critical parameter-matched baseline. VLA-GSE cross-backbone comparison is weak.\n\n- **Reproducibility (1-4): 3.** Architecture and training details are well-specified. Phase 0 data collection pipeline is clear. However, training data generation (especially oracle policy for spatial/physical demonstrations) and hyperparameter choices for LoRA training are underspecified.\n\n- **Overall Rating (1-10, CoRL scale): 6.** Borderline accept. The Phase 0 finding is strong and the experiment coverage is comprehensive. The routing benefit (+6.0% over universal, p = 0.008) is statistically significant but moderate, especially given the 3x parameter increase. The spatial underperformance and missing parameter-matched baseline are significant concerns. If the authors can provide the rank-48 universal LoRA comparison and it confirms that routing provides benefit beyond parameter scaling, this becomes a solid accept. Without it, the core claim remains ambiguous.\n\n- **Confidence (1-5): 4.** I am confident in this assessment. I have expertise in VLA robustness, LoRA-based adaptation, and the LIBERO benchmark family. The review is based on detailed analysis of all three outcome tiers and the full experiment plan."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34451,"cache_read_input_tokens":96862,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":34451,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LQ4Te7gZVbHZuRYt6tuXWZ","session_id":"f3c89458-961d-4d5e-a388-373831b4983c","uuid":"fc9aa09a-837a-4a0b-a897-727c1e7158b4"}
